msgid "Meaning"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:120
msgid "$f$ is defined and continuous on $\\boldsymbol{x}$, and $\\boldsymbol{y}$ is bounded"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:121
msgid "$f$ is defined and continuous on $\\boldsymbol{x}$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:122
msgid "$f$ is defined on $\\boldsymbol{x}$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:123
msgid "nothing is known"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:124
msgid "the value is NaI"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:126
msgid "`Show` prints the lower-case IEEE 1788 names `com`, `dac`, `def`, `trv`, `ill`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:130
msgid "`OverlapState` is the result of `overlap_state`: the IEEE 1788 classification of the relative position of two intervals."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:155
msgid "The constructors correspond to the IEEE 1788 states `bothEmpty`, `firstEmpty`, `secondEmpty`, `before`, `meets`, `overlaps`, `starts`, `containedBy`, `finishes`, `equal`, `after`, `metBy`, `overlappedBy`, `startedBy`, `contains` and `finishedBy`; `OverlapsState`, `EqualIntervals` and `ContainsInterval` carry a suffix only to avoid clashing with method names. `Undefined` is returned only by the decorated version when an operand is NaI."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:164
msgid "`BallContext` describes a target binary format: a precision in bits and an exponent range $[e_{\\min}, e_{\\max}]$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:173
msgid "$e_{\\min}$ and $e_{\\max}$ follow IEEE 754: a finite nonzero value $v$ with $2^{e} \\le |v| < 2^{e+1}$ is in range when $e \\le e_{\\max}$, and it is normal when $e \\ge e_{\\min}$. See [Contexts and flags](#contexts-and-flags)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:179
msgid "`BallFlags` records the conditions raised while rounding an interval into a `BallContext`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:190
msgid "`inexact` is set when an endpoint changed; `overflow` when an endpoint was beyond $e_{\\max}$; `underflow` when an endpoint was below the normal range and its subnormal rounding was inexact. The fields are readable; the accessor methods are listed under [`BallFlags`](#ballflagsnew-ballflagscombine-and-accessors)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:195
#: manual/api/ball_float_checked.md:42
#: manual/api/bin_float_checked.md:43
#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:53
#: manual/api/decimal_gda_checked.md:51
msgid "Construction"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:199
msgid "`BallFloat::new` builds the interval $[c - r, c + r]$ from a center and a radius, enlarged so that it is exact at the requested precision."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:206
msgid "The default precision is the larger of the precisions of `center` and `radius`. With $p$ that precision, the stored interval is"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:215
msgid "which contains $[c - r, c + r]$ (proof in the [design page](../design/ball_float.md#from-midpointradius-to-endpoints)). The endpoints $\\tilde c \\pm R$ are formed exactly, so they may carry more than $p$ bits. Aborts when `center` or `radius` is not finite or when `radius` is negative. A precision below 1 is treated as 1."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:221
msgid "`BallFloat::from_bounds` and `BallFloat::try_from_bounds`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:223
msgid "`BallFloat::from_bounds` builds the interval $[\\underline{x}, \\overline{x}]$ from its endpoints, rounded outward to the requested precision."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:231
msgid "The default precision is the larger of the endpoint precisions. Infinite endpoints build unbounded intervals: `from_bounds(-inf, +inf)` is Entire. The inputs are invalid when an endpoint is NaN, the lower endpoint is $+\\infty$, the upper endpoint is $-\\infty$, or $\\underline{x} > \\overline{x}$; `from_bounds` aborts and `try_from_bounds` returns a domain error. There is no way to build Empty from bounds; use `BallFloat::empty`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:248
msgid "`BallFloat::exact` and `BallFloat::try_exact`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:250
msgid "`BallFloat::exact` builds the singleton $\\{x\\}$ of a finite `BinFloat`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:257
msgid "The default precision is that of `x`. When `x` has more significant bits than the requested precision, the singleton is rounded outward to a two-point interval around `x`. A non-finite `x` aborts `exact` and makes `try_exact` return a domain error."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:262
msgid "`BallFloat::from_int` and `BallFloat::from_coefficient`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:264
msgid "`BallFloat::from_int` and `BallFloat::from_coefficient` build the singleton of an integer."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:272
msgid "The default precision is 16. `from_coefficient` takes a non-negative `BinCoeff` magnitude and a separate sign. The integer is first converted to a `BinFloat` of $\\max(p, 8)$ bits with round-to-nearest, then embedded with `exact`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:277
msgid "[!WARNING] The first conversion rounds to nearest, so when the integer needs more than $\\max(p, 8)$ bits the interval may not contain it: `from_int(257, precision=8)` is $\\{256\\}$. Pass a precision at least as large as the bit length of the integer."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:283
msgid "`BallFloat::from_double`, `BallFloat::from_float` and their `try_` forms"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:285
msgid "These functions build the singleton of the exact binary value of a `Double` or `Float`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:295
msgid "Default precisions are 53 and 24. The conversion to `BinFloat` is exact; a smaller requested precision rounds the singleton outward. NaN and infinities abort the plain forms and are domain errors for the `try_` forms. The result encloses the binary value, not the decimal literal it was written as: `from_double(0.1)` does not contain $1/10$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:301
msgid "`BallFloat::whole` and `BallFloat::empty`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:303
msgid "`BallFloat::whole` returns Entire and `BallFloat::empty` returns Empty."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:310
msgid "The default precision is 53."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:312
msgid "Observers"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:314
msgid "`BallFloat::lower_bound` and `BallFloat::upper_bound`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:316
msgid "`lower_bound` and `upper_bound` return the stored endpoints."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:323
msgid "For an unbounded side the endpoint is an infinity. For Empty they return $+\\infty$ and $-\\infty$ respectively, so test `is_empty` first."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:326
msgid "`BallFloat::center` and `BallFloat::radius`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:328
msgid "`center` and `radius` return the midpoint–radius view of a bounded interval."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:335
msgid "`center` is $(\\underline{x} + \\overline{x})/2$ and `radius` is $(\\overline{x} - \\underline{x})/2$, both computed exactly (the radius is rounded up only if it underflows the exponent range), so $[\\text{center} - \\text{radius}, \\text{center} + \\text{radius}]$ is the stored interval. Both abort on Empty and on unbounded intervals."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:341
msgid "[!NOTE] For bounded intervals whose endpoints are more than about $2^{16}$ binary orders of magnitude apart, `center` is not exact: the smaller endpoint is replaced by a surrogate below the last bit of the larger one (see the [design page](../design/ball_float.md#far-addends-bound-endpoint-sums-by-precision))."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:349
msgid "`midpoint` returns the center rounded to nearest at the interval's precision."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:355
msgid "Entire has midpoint 0. Empty and half-bounded intervals abort."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:357
msgid "`BallFloat::width` and `BallFloat::radius_extended`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:359
msgid "`width` returns $\\operatorname{RU}_p(\\overline{x} - \\underline{x})$ and `radius_extended` returns the radius rounded up to the interval's precision."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:367
msgid "Both return 0 for Empty and $+\\infty$ for unbounded intervals instead of aborting."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:370
msgid "`BallFloat::magnitude` and `BallFloat::mignitude`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:372
msgid "`magnitude` returns $\\max\\{|\\xi| : \\xi \\in \\boldsymbol{x}\\}$ and `mignitude` returns $\\min\\{|\\xi| : \\xi \\in \\boldsymbol{x}\\}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:380
msgid "Both are exact. Empty gives 0; `mignitude` is 0 when the interval contains 0; `magnitude` of an unbounded interval is $+\\infty$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:396
msgid "`BallFloat::precision`, `BallFloat::classify` and `BallFloat::sign`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:398
msgid "`precision` returns the precision tag; `classify` and `sign` describe the interval in the vocabulary of scalar floating-point types."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:407
msgid "`classify` returns `NaN` for Empty, `Finite` for a bounded interval and `Infinity` for an unbounded one. `sign` returns `Positive` when $\\underline{x} > 0$, `Negative` when $\\overline{x} < 0$, and `Zero` otherwise: `Zero` means \"the interval contains 0\", not \"the interval is $\\{0\\}$\". `sign` aborts on Empty."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:413
msgid "Shape predicates"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:415
msgid "`is_empty`, `is_entire`, `is_bounded`, `is_common_interval`, `is_singleton` and `contains_zero` test the shape of the set."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:427
msgid "`is_bounded` and `is_common_interval` (the IEEE 1788 name) are the same: the interval is non-empty with two finite endpoints. `is_singleton` holds when $\\underline{x} = \\overline{x}$. `contains_zero` is false for Empty."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:431
#: manual/design/decimal_gda.md:417
msgid "Precision"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:435
msgid "`with_precision` re-rounds an interval to a new precision without shrinking it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:442
msgid "For a bounded interval the center is rounded with `mode`, the displacement is added to the radius, and the result is rebuilt as in `BallFloat::new`. For an unbounded interval the finite endpoint is rounded outward and `mode` is ignored. Empty stays Empty with the new precision. The result always contains the input, except in the extreme case noted under `center` when the new precision exceeds about $2^{16}$ bits."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:451
msgid "`normalized` rebuilds a bounded interval from its normalized center and radius (`BinFloat::normalized` removes trailing zero bits), and re-rounds the finite endpoints of an unbounded interval outward."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:459
msgid "The result contains the input."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:461
msgid "Set operations"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:463
msgid "`BallFloat::intersection` and `BallFloat::convex_hull`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:465
msgid "`intersection` returns $\\boldsymbol{x} \\cap \\boldsymbol{y}$ and `convex_hull` returns the smallest interval containing $\\boldsymbol{x} \\cup \\boldsymbol{y}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:473
msgid "Both are computed by endpoint `max`/`min` and are exact apart from the final outward rounding to the larger precision. Disjoint intervals intersect to Empty; Empty is the identity of `convex_hull`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:477
msgid "`BallFloat::cancel_plus` and `BallFloat::cancel_minus`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:479
msgid "`cancel_minus(x, y)` returns the interval $\\boldsymbol{z}$ with $\\boldsymbol{y} + \\boldsymbol{z} = \\boldsymbol{x}$, the inverse of addition used to undo a previous sum; `cancel_plus(x, y)` is `cancel_minus(x, -y)`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:488
msgid "For bounded operands `cancel_minus` is $[\\underline{x} - \\underline{y}, \\overline{x} - \\overline{y}]$, rounded outward. When that is not an interval (the width of $\\boldsymbol{y}$ exceeds the width of $\\boldsymbol{x}$), when an operand is unbounded, or when only $\\boldsymbol{y}$ is empty, the result is Entire. Empty $\\boldsymbol{x}$ with bounded or empty $\\boldsymbol{y}$ gives Empty."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:509
msgid "Relations"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:511
msgid "All relations are set relations of IEEE 1788; none of them is a total order. They never abort."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:516
msgid "`contains` tests whether a point belongs to the interval."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:522
msgid "It returns false for Empty and for a non-finite point (an unbounded interval contains all sufficiently large reals, but not $\\pm\\infty$ or NaN). The trait method `@lf_arith.Contains::contains` instead takes two intervals and tests inclusion; see [Trait implementations](#trait-implementations)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:529
msgid "These test inclusion, inclusion in the interior, equality and disjointness of sets."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:539
msgid "`x.subset(y)` is $\\boldsymbol{x} \\subseteq \\boldsymbol{y}$ and `x.interior(y)` is $\\boldsymbol{x} \\subseteq \\operatorname{int} \\boldsymbol{y}$, where an infinite endpoint counts as interior (so Entire is interior to itself). Empty is a subset of, interior to and disjoint from every interval. `set_equal` ignores the precision tag and the representation of the endpoints."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:548
msgid "`overlaps` tests whether two intervals share a point; `maybe_eq` is the same relation read as \"the two unknown points may be equal\"; `separated_from` is its negation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:558
msgid "`overlaps` is false and `separated_from` is true when an operand is Empty."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:562
msgid "These hold when the order holds for every pair of points."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:570
msgid "`x.definitely_lt(y)` is $\\overline{x} < \\underline{y}$, `definitely_le` is $\\overline{x} \\le \\underline{y}$ and `definitely_gt` is $\\underline{x} > \\overline{y}$. All three are false when an operand is Empty (unlike `precedes`, which is vacuously true)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:577
msgid "These are the order relations of IEEE 1788."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:586
msgid "For non-empty operands:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:588
msgid "Relation"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:588
msgid "Condition"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:590
msgid "$\\underline{x} \\le \\underline{y}$ and $\\overline{x} \\le \\overline{y}$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:591
msgid "$\\underline{x} < \\underline{y}$ (or both $-\\infty$) and $\\overline{x} < \\overline{y}$ (or both $+\\infty$)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:595
msgid "With Empty: `less` and `strictly_less` hold only when both are Empty; `precedes` and `strictly_precedes` hold when either is Empty."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:600
msgid "`overlap_state` classifies the relative position of two intervals."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:606
msgid "The result is one of the sixteen states of [`OverlapState`](#overlapstate) other than `Undefined`; it is computed from the comparisons of the four endpoints."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:626
#: manual/api/ball_float_checked.md:201
#: manual/api/bin_float_checked.md:163
msgid "Arithmetic"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:630
msgid "The four basic operations return the outward-rounded hull of $\\{\\xi \\circ \\eta : \\xi \\in \\boldsymbol{x}, \\eta \\in \\boldsymbol{y}\\}$; they are also available as the operators `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:641
msgid "The result precision is the larger operand precision; an Empty operand gives Empty. Endpoint formulas:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:652
msgid "with $0 \\cdot \\infty$ taken as 0 in $S$ (a zero endpoint times an unbounded side contributes 0). For bounded operands the sign of the operands selects the two products that can be extremal, so at most two (four when both operands contain 0) products are evaluated."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:657
msgid ""
"Division follows IEEE 1788: $\\boldsymbol{x} / \\boldsymbol{y}$ is the hull of $\\{\\xi/\\eta : \\eta \\n"
"e 0\\}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:660
msgid "Divisor $\\boldsymbol{y}$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:660
#: manual/api/bin_float.md:35
#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:257
#: manual/conformance/bin_float.md:71
#: manual/performance_audit.md:122
msgid "Result"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:662
msgid "$[\\operatorname{RD}\\min Q, \\operatorname{RU}\\max Q]$ over the endpoint quotients $Q$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:663
msgid "Empty"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:664
msgid "Entire"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:665
msgid "$[0, \\overline{y}]$ or $[\\underline{y}, 0]$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:665
msgid "half-unbounded (see below), or Entire when $\\underline{x} < 0 < \\overline{x}$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:667
msgid "When the divisor touches 0 at one end, the quotient is unbounded on one side: for example $[1, 2]/[0, 4] = [1/4, +\\infty)$ and $[-2, -1]/[0, 4] = (-\\infty, -1/4]$. A dividend equal to $\\{0\\}$ gives $\\{0\\}$ for any divisor other than $\\{0\\}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:686
msgid "`BallFloat::neg` and `BallFloat::abs`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:688
msgid "`neg` returns $[-\\overline{x}, -\\underline{x}]$ and `abs` returns $\\{|\\xi| : \\xi \\in \\boldsymbol{x}\\}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:696
msgid "Both are exact. `neg` is also the unary operator `-`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:700
msgid "`reciprocal` returns $1/\\boldsymbol{x}$ with the division rules above."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:706
msgid "`BallFloat::square` and `BallFloat::pown`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:708
msgid "`square` returns $\\{\\xi^2\\}$ and `pown` returns $\\{\\xi^n\\}$ for an integer exponent $n$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:716
msgid "Unlike `x * x`, these use the same point twice, so `[-1, 2].square()` is $[0, 4]$ (while `x * x` is $[-2, 4]$). `pown` evaluates the monotone pieces of $\\xi^n$ at the endpoints with directed rounding: odd positive powers are increasing; even positive powers decrease then increase, with minimum 0 when $0 \\in \\boldsymbol{x}$; negative powers have a pole at 0. `pown(x, 0)` is $\\{1\\}$ for every non-empty `x`; Empty stays Empty. `pown(x, n)` with $n < 0$ returns Empty for $\\boldsymbol{x} = \\{0\\}$, a half-unbounded interval when 0 is an endpoint, and for 0 in the interior Entire (odd $n$) or $[\\min(\\underline{x}^n, \\overline{x}^n), +\\infty)$ (even $n$)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:740
msgid "`fma(x, y, z)` returns an enclosure of $\\{\\xi\\eta + \\zeta\\}$ with a single outward rounding."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:747
msgid "The product bounds are computed as for `mul` and added to the endpoints of $\\boldsymbol{z}$ before the final rounding, so the result is never wider than `x * y + z`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:751
msgid "`BallFloat::minimum` and `BallFloat::maximum`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:753
msgid "`minimum` and `maximum` return $\\{\\min(\\xi, \\eta)\\}$ and $\\{\\max(\\xi, \\eta)\\}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:761
msgid "`minimum` is $[\\min(\\underline{x}, \\underline{y}), \\min(\\overline{x}, \\overline{y})]$ and `maximum` is the analogue with `max`. An Empty operand gives Empty."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:765
#: manual/api/ball_float_checked.md:285
#: manual/api/bin_float.md:1386
#: manual/api/bin_float_checked.md:361
#: manual/api/decimal.md:1203
#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:298
#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:132
#: manual/design/decimal_gda.md:422
msgid "Elementary functions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:767
msgid "Each function $f$ returns an interval containing $f(\\boldsymbol{x} \\cap D_f)$, where $D_f$ is the domain of $f$; points of $\\boldsymbol{x}$ outside $D_f$ are ignored and the result is Empty when $\\boldsymbol{x} \\cap D_f$ is empty. The result precision is the argument's precision."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:773
msgid "Functions come in two forms. The total form (`exp_interval`, `sin_interval`, …) always returns a valid enclosure; when the certified evaluation exhausts its refinement budget it returns a wider, still valid, interval. The `try_` form (`try_exp_interval`, …) returns `Err(ArithmeticError)` in that case, with a certification-failure detail naming the operation, stage and reason. Except where noted, the `try_` forms evaluate each endpoint with the corresponding `bin_float` `try_*_ctx` function rounded toward $-\\infty$ or $+\\infty$; the total forms either call them or use the certified series of this package. Both forms return the same set on ordinary inputs; their bounds may differ by an ulp."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:786
msgid "`sqrt_interval` returns $\\sqrt{\\boldsymbol{x} \\cap [0, +\\infty)}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:792
msgid "The endpoints are the downward and upward square roots at the interval's precision. Empty when $\\overline{x} < 0$. There is no `try_` form: square root never fails."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:796
msgid "Exponentials"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:798
msgid "`exp_interval`, `exp2_interval`, `exp10_interval` and `expm1_interval` return enclosures of $e^{\\xi}$, $2^{\\xi}$, $10^{\\xi}$ and $e^{\\xi} - 1$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:812
msgid "All four are increasing, so the result is $[\\operatorname{RD} f(\\underline{x}), \\operatorname{RU} f(\\overline{x})]$. `exp_interval` uses a certified Taylor series with argument halving and never needs a fallback; for $|\\xi| \\ge 2^{30}$ it returns $[\\text{largest finite}, +\\infty)$ or $[0, \\text{smallest positive}]$. `exp2_interval` and `exp10_interval` evaluate $e^{\\xi \\ln b}$ at 96 extra bits and return exact powers for integer endpoints (for `exp10_interval`, exponents $0 \\le n \\le 100000$). The total `expm1_interval` falls back to $[-1, +\\infty)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:822
msgid "Logarithms"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:824
msgid "`ln_interval`, `log2_interval`, `log10_interval` and `log1p_interval` return enclosures of $\\ln \\xi$, $\\log_2 \\xi$, $\\log_{10} \\xi$ and $\\ln(1 + \\xi)$ over their domains $(0, \\infty)$ and $(-1, \\infty)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:839
msgid "When the interval reaches the domain boundary ($\\underline{x} \\le 0$, or $\\underline{x} \\le -1$ for `log1p`) the lower endpoint is $-\\infty$; when it lies entirely outside the domain the result is Empty. `log10_interval` returns exact integers at endpoints $10^k$, $0 \\le k \\le 9$. The total `log1p_interval` falls back to Entire."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:856
msgid "`BallFloat::pow_interval` and `BallFloat::try_pow_interval`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:858
msgid "`pow_interval(x, y)` returns an enclosure of $\\{\\xi^{\\eta} : \\xi \\in \\boldsymbol{x}, \\eta \\in \\boldsymbol{y}\\}$ over the IEEE 1788 domain of `pow`: $\\xi > 0$, or $\\xi = 0$ and $\\eta > 0$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:867
msgid "Negative parts of the base are ignored (use `pown` or `rootn` for negative bases). The result is the hull of the four corner values, extended by 1 when the base interval contains 1 or the exponent interval contains 0, by 0 when the base reaches 0 with positive exponents, and by $+\\infty$ when the base reaches 0 with negative exponents. $0^{\\eta}$ for $\\eta \\le 0$ is excluded, so `pow_interval([0, 0], y)` is Empty when $\\overline{y} \\le 0$. The result precision is the larger operand precision. The total form falls back to an evaluation of $e^{\\eta \\ln \\xi}$ at 192 extra bits."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:876
msgid "`BallFloat::rootn` and `BallFloat::try_rootn`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:878
msgid "`rootn(x, n)` returns an enclosure of the real $n$-th roots $\\{\\xi^{1/n}\\}$: for even $n$ over $\\xi \\ge 0$, for odd $n$ over all reals, and for negative $n$ the reciprocal of the root."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:887
msgid "`rootn(x, 0)` and `rootn(x, Int min)` are Empty; `try_rootn(x, 0)` is a domain error. `rootn(x, 1)` is `x` and `rootn(x, 2)` is `sqrt_interval`. The total form evaluates other degrees through `pow_interval` with an enclosure of $1/n$, so it may be slightly wider than `try_rootn`. For negative $n$, `try_rootn` returns Entire when an odd root's argument contains 0."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:893
msgid "`BallFloat::hypot` and `BallFloat::try_hypot`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:895
msgid "`hypot` returns an enclosure of $\\{\\sqrt{\\xi^2 + \\eta^2}\\}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:902
msgid "The function is increasing in $|\\xi|$ and $|\\eta|$, so the result is evaluated at the endpoints of `abs(x)` and `abs(y)`. The total form falls back to `sqrt_interval(square(x) + square(y))`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:906
#: manual/api/bin_float_checked.md:401
msgid "Trigonometric functions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:908
msgid "`sin_interval`, `cos_interval` and `tan_interval` return enclosures of $\\sin$, $\\cos$ and $\\tan$ over the interval (in radians)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:920
msgid "Both forms reduce each endpoint by a certified enclosure of $\\pi/2$ and evaluate certified Taylor series. A critical point $k\\pi/2$ inside the interval contributes the extremum $\\pm 1$ of `sin`/`cos`; for `tan` an odd multiple of $\\pi/2$ inside the interval (a pole) makes the result Entire. Unbounded arguments give $[-1, 1]$ (Entire for `tan`). When the larger endpoint magnitude is at least $2^{\\max(65536,\\, 4p) + 1}$, the total forms return $[-1, 1]$ (Entire) without evaluating and the `try_` forms return a resource-limit error; the total forms use the same fallback when the 12 refinement steps are exhausted."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:930
msgid "Trigonometric functions of $\\pi x$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:932
msgid "`sinpi_interval`, `cospi_interval` and `tanpi_interval` return enclosures of $\\sin \\pi\\xi$, $\\cos \\pi\\xi$ and $\\tan \\pi\\xi$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:944
msgid "Here the critical points are the exact half-integers $k/2$, located without approximating $\\pi$. `tanpi_interval` returns a half-unbounded interval when a pole is exactly an endpoint (for example $[1/2, 1]$ gives $(-\\infty, 0]$), Empty for the singleton of a pole, and Entire when a pole lies inside. Unbounded arguments and fallbacks give $[-1, 1]$ (Entire for `tanpi`)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:964
msgid "Inverse trigonometric functions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:966
msgid "`asin_interval`, `acos_interval` and `atan_interval` return enclosures of $\\arcsin$, $\\arccos$ (over $[-1, 1]$) and $\\arctan$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:978
msgid "`asin` and `atan` are increasing and `acos` is decreasing, so only endpoints are evaluated. The total `atan_interval` uses a certified series with a Machin-formula enclosure of $\\pi$; `asin` is computed as $\\arctan(\\xi/\\sqrt{1 - \\xi^2})$ and `acos` as $\\pi/2 - \\arcsin \\xi$, at 64 extra bits. `atan` of an infinite endpoint is $\\pm\\pi/2$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:984
msgid "`BallFloat::atan2_interval` and `BallFloat::try_atan2_interval`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:986
msgid ""
"`y.atan2_interval(x)` returns an enclosure of the angles $\\operatorname{atan2}(\\eta, \\xi) \\in (-\\pi, \\pi]$ of the points $(\\xi, \\eta) \\n"
"e (0, 0)$ of the box $\\boldsymbol{x} \\times \\boldsymbol{y}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:995
msgid "The receiver is the ordinate. The result is the hull of the angles at the four corners and at the points where the box meets the axes. When the box crosses the branch cut (negative $\\xi$, and $\\eta$ ranging over negative values and 0) the result is $[-\\pi, \\pi]$. The box $\\{(0, 0)\\}$ gives Empty. The total form falls back to $[-\\pi, \\pi]$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1001
#: manual/api/bin_float_checked.md:435
msgid "Hyperbolic functions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1003
msgid "`sinh_interval`, `cosh_interval`, `tanh_interval`, `asinh_interval`, `acosh_interval` and `atanh_interval` return enclosures of the hyperbolic functions and their inverses over their domains ($[1, \\infty)$ for `acosh`, $(-1, 1)$ for `atanh`)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1023
msgid "`cosh` has its minimum 1 at 0; the others are monotone. The total forms evaluate the defining formulas ($(e^\\xi - e^{-\\xi})/2$, $\\ln(\\xi + \\sqrt{\\xi^2 + 1})$, $\\tfrac12 \\ln\\frac{1+\\xi}{1-\\xi}$, …) in interval arithmetic at 192 extra bits at each endpoint, so they never fail; `tanh_interval` is clipped to $[-1, 1]$. `atanh` of an interval reaching $\\pm 1$ is unbounded on that side."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1042
msgid "Contexts and flags"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1044
msgid "`BallContext::new` and `BallContext::try_new`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1046
msgid "`BallContext::new` builds a context from a precision and an exponent range."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1053
msgid "The defaults are those of binary64: precision 53, $e_{\\min} = -1022$, $e_{\\max} = 1023$. A precision below 1 or $e_{\\min} > e_{\\max}$ aborts `new` and is a domain error for `try_new`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1057
msgid "`BallContext::binary32` and `BallContext::binary64`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1059
msgid "These return the contexts of the IEEE 754 binary32 ($p = 24$, $[-126, 127]$) and binary64 ($p = 53$, $[-1022, 1023]$) formats."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1069
msgid "These return the parameters of a context."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1077
msgid "`BallFlags::new`, `BallFlags::combine` and accessors"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1079
msgid "`BallFlags::new` returns the flags with nothing raised; `combine` is their union; the accessors read the fields."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1092
msgid "`apply_ctx` rounds an interval outward into a context and reports the flags."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1098
msgid "Each finite nonzero endpoint is rounded outward to the context precision. An endpoint whose rounded exponent exceeds $e_{\\max}$ overflows: it becomes $\\mp\\infty$ if it is a negative lower or a positive upper endpoint, and the largest finite value of the right sign otherwise (a positive lower endpoint becomes the largest finite value, which is still below it). An endpoint below the normal range is rounded outward on the subnormal grid $2^{e_{\\min} - p + 1}\\mathbb{Z}$, so a tiny positive upper endpoint becomes the smallest subnormal rather than 0. Zeros and infinities are kept. Empty gives Empty with no flags. The result has the context precision."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1110
msgid "These compute the operation and then apply the context."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1119
msgid "`x.add_ctx(y, ctx)` is `(x + y).apply_ctx(ctx)`. When the operands' precision is at least the context precision, the double rounding gives the same endpoints as a single outward rounding into the context (see the [design page](../design/ball_float.md#contexts-and-double-rounding))."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1124
msgid "`BallFloat::exp_ctx` and `BallFloat::ln_ctx`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1126
msgid "`exp_ctx` and `ln_ctx` evaluate `exp_interval` and `ln_interval` at 32 bits more than the context precision and then apply the context."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1136
msgid "`midpoint_ctx` returns the center rounded to nearest at the context precision."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1142
msgid "Subnormal results are rounded on the subnormal grid and raise `underflow` when inexact; `inexact` is set when the center changed. The exponent upper limit is not applied, so `overflow` is never raised. Entire gives 0; Empty and half-bounded intervals abort."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1163
msgid "Decorated intervals"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1165
msgid "A decorated operation computes the bare result and the decoration $d = \\min(d_1, \\dots, d_k, d_f)$, where $d_i$ are the operand decorations and $d_f$ is the decoration of the operation on these operands (`Com` when the function is defined and continuous on the whole input box, `Trv` when the input leaves the domain, as listed below). The minimum is then made canonical (Empty → `Trv`, unbounded with `Com` → `Dac`). Any NaI operand gives NaI."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1174
msgid "`BallFloatDecorated::new` decorates a bare interval."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1180
msgid "The default decoration is `Com`. The decoration is made canonical, and `Ill` is replaced by `Trv`: `new` never builds NaI."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1183
msgid "`BallFloatDecorated::nai` and `BallFloatDecorated::is_nai`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1185
msgid "`nai` returns NaI, the result of an invalid decorated construction; `is_nai` tests for it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1193
msgid "NaI has decoration `Ill` and an Empty interval (default precision 53), but it is not Empty: `is_empty` is false for NaI."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1196
msgid "`BallFloatDecorated::interval` and `BallFloatDecorated::decoration`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1198
msgid "These return the bare interval and the decoration."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1205
msgid "Decorated predicates and relations"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1207
msgid "The predicates and relations of `BallFloatDecorated` apply the bare relation to the intervals and return false when an operand is NaI."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1227
msgid "`overlap_state` returns `Undefined` when an operand is NaI."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1229
msgid "Decorated set operations"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1231
msgid "`intersection`, `convex_hull`, `cancel_plus` and `cancel_minus` apply the bare operation and always lower the decoration to `Trv` (set operations are not point functions)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1242
msgid "Decorated arithmetic"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1244
msgid "The arithmetic operations apply the bare operation; their operation decoration is `Com` except where the table says otherwise."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1263
#: manual/design/bin_float_checked.md:122
#: manual/design/decimal_gda.md:119
msgid "Operation"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1263
msgid "Operation decoration"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1265
msgid "`Trv` when the divisor contains 0"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1266
msgid "`Trv` when $n < 0$ and $0 \\in \\boldsymbol{x}$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1267
msgid "identity (IEEE 1788 `pos`)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1268
msgid "others"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1270
msgid "Decorated elementary functions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1272
msgid "The decorated elementary functions apply the bare total form (there are no decorated `try_` forms) and lower the decoration when the input leaves the domain of the function."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1307
#: manual/api/decimal.md:1323
#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:136
msgid "Function"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1307
msgid "Operation decoration `Trv` when"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1309
msgid "$\\underline{x} < 0$ (or the input is Empty)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1314
msgid "$\\underline{x} \\le -1$ or $\\overline{x} \\ge 1$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1315
msgid "$n = 0$, or $n$ even and $\\underline{x} < 0$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1316
msgid "$\\underline{x} < 0$, or $0 \\in \\boldsymbol{x}$ and $\\underline{y} \\le 0$, or the result is Empty"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1317
msgid "the result is Entire (a pole may lie inside)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1318
msgid "both operands contain 0"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1320
msgid "All other functions have operation decoration `Com`. For `y.atan2_interval(x)` the decoration is `Def` when the box crosses the branch cut ($\\underline{x} < 0$, $\\underline{y} < 0 \\le \\overline{y}$) and `Dac` when it touches the cut from above ($\\overline{x} < 0$, $\\underline{y} = 0$)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1325
msgid "[!WARNING] `rootn` with a negative degree does not lower the decoration when 0 is in the argument, although $\\xi^{1/n}$ is undefined at 0 for $n < 0$: `rootn([0, 4], -2)` is decorated `dac` instead of `trv`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1332
msgid "`apply_ctx` applies `BallFloat::apply_ctx` to the interval and keeps the decoration (made canonical again, so an overflowed `Com` becomes `Dac`)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float.md:1339
msgid "NaI stays NaI, with the context precision."
msgstr ""

