#: manual/api/ball_float_checked.md:1
msgid "`ball_float_checked` API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float_checked.md:3
msgid "`ball_float_checked` provides `BallFloatResult`, a closed wrapper around `Result[BallFloat, ArithmeticError]` for interval computations. Construction validates its inputs, every operation passes on the first error, and the interval operations of [`ball_float`](ball_float.md) are applied when all operands are successes. A valid but uninformative enclosure (the empty set, the whole line) is a success, not an error. The wrapper stores no decorations and no `BallFlags`. The [tutorial](../tutorial/ball_float_checked.md) builds pipelines; the [design page](../design/ball_float_checked.md) explains which outcomes are errors and why."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float_checked.md:13
msgid "The examples print a result with this helper (`to_string` of a bounded ball is `centre +/- radius` in exact binary notation, `mpe` meaning $m \\cdot 2^{e}$):"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float_checked.md:27
#: manual/api/bin_float_checked.md:27
msgid "The wrapper type"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float_checked.md:31
msgid "`BallFloatResult` is either a successful `BallFloat` or an `ArithmeticError`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float_checked.md:39
msgid "The single private field holds a `Result[@ball_float.BallFloat, @arithmetic.ArithmeticError]`. The type has no `Eq` or `Show`; use `result()`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float_checked.md:46
msgid "These functions wrap an existing interval, error or `Result` unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float_checked.md:56
msgid "These constructors build a point interval from an integer through `BallFloat::from_int` / `BallFloat::from_coefficient`; they never fail."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float_checked.md:64
msgid "The default precision is **16 bits**. The integer is first converted to a `BinFloat` at $\\max(\\textit{precision}, 8)$ bits with nearest rounding and then enclosed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float_checked.md:68
msgid "[!WARNING] On the current branch the first conversion rounds, so an integer that needs more bits than the precision is not enclosed: `from_int(100001)` (17 bits) is the point $100000$. Pass a precision large enough for the integer, or use `from_bounds`, which rounds outward."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float_checked.md:76
msgid "These constructors enclose a finite `Double` or `Float`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float_checked.md:83
msgid "The source is converted exactly (at least 53 or 24 bits) and then rounded outward to `precision` (defaults 53 and 24), so the result always contains the source value. A NaN or infinite source gives a `DomainError` (`ball double source must be finite`, `ball float source must be finite`)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float_checked.md:90
msgid "`exact(x, precision~)` encloses a finite `BinFloat`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float_checked.md:96
msgid "`precision` defaults to `x.precision()`. The bounds are `x` rounded down and up to that precision, so the result contains `x`. A non-finite `x` gives a `DomainError` (`ball exact source must be finite`)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float_checked.md:102
msgid "`from_bounds(lo, hi, precision~)` builds the interval $[\\textit{lo}, \\textit{hi}]$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float_checked.md:109
msgid "`precision` defaults to the larger bound precision; `lo` is rounded down and `hi` up. It returns a `DomainError` when a bound is NaN, when `lo` is $+\\infty$, when `hi` is $-\\infty$ (IEEE 1788 intervals are subsets of $\\mathbb{R}$, so $[+\\infty, +\\infty]$ is not an interval), or when `lo > hi`. Infinite bounds on the open side are allowed: $[-\\infty, 0]$ is valid."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float_checked.md:118
msgid "`whole(precision~)` is the entire real line $[-\\infty, +\\infty]$ (default precision 53); it never fails."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float_checked.md:152
#: manual/api/bin_float_checked.md:81
#: manual/api/decimal_checked.md:134
#: manual/api/decimal_gda_checked.md:87
msgid "Observation"
