#: manual/api/ball_float_checked.md:168
#: manual/api/bin_float_checked.md:96
msgid "Composition"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float_checked.md:172
msgid "`map(f)` applies an infallible interval function to a success; `bind(f)` applies one that may fail. Errors pass through unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float_checked.md:180
msgid "They satisfy the monad and functor laws proved in the [`bin_float_checked` design](../design/bin_float_checked.md#the-monad-laws); the proof does not depend on the value type."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float_checked.md:184
#: manual/api/bin_float_checked.md:144
msgid "Unary value maps"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float_checked.md:188
msgid "These methods are `map` of the `BallFloat` method of the same name."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float_checked.md:197
msgid "`neg` and `abs` are the exact interval images $\\{-t\\}$ and $\\{|t|\\}$; `with_precision` returns an enclosure of the input at the new precision for every rounding mode (the mode only steers the centre)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float_checked.md:205
msgid "These methods combine two wrappers with the `BallFloat` operators."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float_checked.md:214
msgid "The left operand's error comes first, then the right one's. Interval arithmetic itself never fails: the result encloses $\\{\\, s \\circ t : s \\in X, t \\in Y \\,\\}$. Division by an interval that is exactly $\\{0\\}$ gives the empty set, and division by an interval that contains zero in its interior gives the whole line."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float_checked.md:222
msgid "These methods raise an interval to an integer power through the Luna-Flow/arithmetic traits `PowNatChecked` and `PowIntChecked`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float_checked.md:230
msgid "The context is `ArithmeticContext::new(x.precision())`; `ball_float` uses only its precision. The result is the enclosure of $\\{t^{n}\\}$, computed as a power (so `pow_int(2)` of $[1, 3]$ is $[1, 9]$, tighter than `x * x` would be for an interval containing zero). A zero base with a negative exponent gives the empty set."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float_checked.md:238
msgid "`rootn(n)` encloses the real $n$-th root with `BallFloat::try_rootn`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float_checked.md:244
msgid "Degree $0$ gives a `DomainError`. Even roots ignore the negative part of the interval (set semantics): the root of $[-1, 1]$ is $[0, 1]$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float_checked.md:249
msgid "These binary functions enclose $x^{y}$, $\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$ and the angle of $(\\text{abscissa}, \\text{self})$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float_checked.md:258
msgid "They call `try_pow_interval`, `try_hypot` and `try_atan2_interval`. Operand errors are taken left first; the operation can fail with a `CertificationFailure`. Points outside the real domain are dropped from the result set, so $(-2)^{0.5}$ is the empty interval."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float_checked.md:287
msgid "Exponentials, logarithms, trigonometric and hyperbolic functions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float_checked.md:289
msgid "`exp`, `exp2`, `exp10`, `expm1`, `ln`, `log2`, `log10`, `log1p`, `sin`, `cos`, `tan`, `sinpi`, `cospi`, `tanpi`, `asin`, `acos`, `atan`, `sinh`, `cosh`, `tanh`, `asinh`, `acosh` and `atanh` enclose the image of the interval under the function."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float_checked.md:320
msgid "Each method is `bind` of `BallFloat::try_<name>_interval` at the interval's precision. The result $Y$ satisfies $f(X \\cap \\operatorname{dom} f) \\subseteq Y$; parts of $X$ outside the domain are ignored, so $\\ln([-1, 1]) = [-\\infty, 0]$ and $\\ln([-2, -2])$ is empty. The only error is a `CertificationFailure` when an enclosure cannot be certified, for example the cosine of $2^{70000}$ (reason `ResourceLimit`). There are no context variants; precision is set with `with_precision`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/ball_float_checked.md:360
#: manual/api/bin_float_checked.md:477
msgid "The operators `+`, `-`, `*`, `/` and unary `-` call `add`, `sub`, `mul`, `div` and `neg`."
