#: manual/api/numeric_expr.md:1
msgid "numeric_expr API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/numeric_expr.md:3
msgid "`numeric_expr` is a small expression language for numeric test corpora and tools. An `Expr` is a tree whose leaves are literals (raw source text) and whose inner nodes apply a named operation to argument expressions. The package does not know any number type: `evaluate` folds the tree with two callbacks supplied by the caller, one that decodes a literal and one that executes an operation. Every node carries a `SourceSpan` so that a failure can be reported at its source line. The [tutorial](../tutorial/numeric_expr.md) builds and evaluates expressions step by step, and the [design page](../design/numeric_expr.md) states the evaluation semantics and proves its invariants."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/numeric_expr.md:25
msgid "`SourceSpan` records where a syntax node came from: a source name (usually a file path), a line and a column."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/numeric_expr.md:34
msgid "The fields are private and immutable. Two spans are equal when the source, line and column are all equal."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/numeric_expr.md:39
msgid "`SourceSpan::new(source, line?, column?)` creates a span."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/numeric_expr.md:45
msgid "`line` and `column` default to `0`, which means \"unknown\". The values are stored as given; no range check is made."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/numeric_expr.md:50
msgid "These accessors return the three components of a span."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/numeric_expr.md:70
msgid "Syntax nodes"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/numeric_expr.md:74
msgid "`Literal` is a leaf of an expression: the raw text of an operand, such as `\"1.20\"`, `\"-Inf\"` or `\"#7C00016E\"`, together with its span."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/numeric_expr.md:83
msgid "The text is not parsed when the literal is built. Its meaning is decided by the `decode` callback of `evaluate`, so the same tree can be read as a binary, decimal or interval value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/numeric_expr.md:89
msgid "`Literal::new(raw, span?)` builds a literal; `raw` and `span` read it back."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/numeric_expr.md:97
#: manual/api/numeric_expr.md:125
msgid "The default span is `SourceSpan::new(\"\")`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/numeric_expr.md:101
msgid "`Operation` names the operation of an inner node, for example `\"add\"` or `\"squareroot\"`, together with its span."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/numeric_expr.md:110
msgid "The package attaches no meaning to the name and does not record an arity. The `invoke` callback of `evaluate` decides which names exist and how many arguments each one takes."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/numeric_expr.md:116
msgid "`Operation::new(name, span?)` builds an operation; `name` and `span` read it back."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/numeric_expr.md:127
msgid "Expressions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/numeric_expr.md:131
msgid "`Expr` is an immutable expression tree."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/numeric_expr.md:139
msgid "The representation is private. Only the two constructors below create expressions, so every `Expr` has the shape"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/numeric_expr.md:146
msgid "`Expr` has no equality, `Debug` or `Show` implementation; inspect an expression by evaluating it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/numeric_expr.md:151
msgid "`Expr::literal(literal)` is the leaf expression for one literal."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/numeric_expr.md:159
msgid "`Expr::invoke(operation, arguments)` applies `operation` to the argument expressions, in order."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/numeric_expr.md:166
msgid "The array is copied into the tree, so later changes to `arguments` do not change the expression. An empty array is allowed and gives a nullary operation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/numeric_expr.md:204
msgid "Evaluation"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/numeric_expr.md:208
msgid "`evaluate(expression, decode, invoke)` computes the value of `expression` bottom-up with the two callbacks."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/numeric_expr.md:215
msgid "`V` is the value type and `E` the error type of the callbacks. The rules are:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/numeric_expr.md:217
msgid "a literal $\\ell$ evaluates to `decode(ℓ)`; an `Err(e)` becomes `Err(LiteralFailure(ℓ, e))`;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/numeric_expr.md:219
msgid "an invocation $o(e_1, \\dots, e_n)$ first evaluates $e_1, \\dots, e_n$ from left to right. The first argument that fails ends the evaluation and its error is returned unchanged. When all arguments succeed with values $v_1, \\dots, v_n$, the result is `invoke(o, [v1, ..., vn])`, and an `Err(e)` becomes `Err(OperationFailure(o, e))`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/numeric_expr.md:225
msgid "Consequently the callbacks are called in post-order (children left to right, then the parent), each node at most once, and evaluation stops at the first failure. On success `decode` runs once per literal leaf and `invoke` once per invocation node. The package performs no other effect. The recursion depth equals the height of the tree. The [design page](../design/numeric_expr.md) proves these properties."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/numeric_expr.md:232
msgid "`evaluate` never aborts on its own; any abort comes from the callbacks."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/numeric_expr.md:236
msgid "`EvalError[E]` tells which node failed and carries the callback's error."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/numeric_expr.md:246
msgid "`LiteralFailure(literal, error)`: `decode(literal)` returned `Err(error)`. `literal.span()` locates the operand."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/numeric_expr.md:248
msgid "`OperationFailure(operation, error)`: `invoke` returned `Err(error)` for `operation`, after all its arguments evaluated successfully."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/numeric_expr.md:250
msgid "`UnsupportedExpression(span)`: the tree contains a form the evaluator does not handle. Trees built with `Expr::literal` and `Expr::invoke` never produce it; the constructor is reserved for forms the private representation can hold but the public constructors cannot yet build, such as variables and binders."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/numeric_expr.md:291
