#: manual/api/semantic.md:1
msgid "`semantic` API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/semantic.md:3
msgid "`semantic` projects concrete values of `floating` onto a small representation-independent model: an exact reduced rational, a signed infinity, NaN, a closed interval of such scalars, and a short error vocabulary. Two values of different representations (for example a binary64 `BinFloat` and an IEEE `Decimal`) project to equal semantic values exactly when they denote the same number. The package performs no arithmetic and no rounding. The [tutorial](../tutorial/semantic.md) shows typical comparisons; the [design page](../design/semantic.md) derives why the projection is exact and what it forgets."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/semantic.md:13
msgid "The examples assume this helper, which prints a semantic scalar as `numerator/denominator`:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/semantic.md:32
msgid "`ExactRational` is an exact rational number kept in lowest terms with a positive denominator."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/semantic.md:41
msgid "The fields are private, so every value is built by `ExactRational::new` or `ExactRational::from_scaled_integer` and satisfies the invariant"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/semantic.md:48
msgid "Because this form is unique for every rational number, the derived `==` is equality of rational numbers."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/semantic.md:53
msgid "`new(numerator, denominator)` builds the rational $n/d$ and reduces it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/semantic.md:59
msgid "A negative denominator moves its sign to the numerator; both parts are divided by their greatest common divisor (Euclid's algorithm on `BigInt`); zero becomes $0/1$. **Aborts** when `denominator` is zero."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/semantic.md:65
msgid "`from_scaled_integer(significand, radix, exponent)` builds the exact value $m \\cdot r^{e}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/semantic.md:72
msgid "For $e \\ge 0$ the result is $(m r^{e})/1$; for $e < 0$ it is $m / r^{-e}$, reduced. **Aborts** when `radix` $\\le 1$. The power is computed exactly, so the size of the result grows linearly with $|e|$ (about $|e| \\log_2 r$ bits)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/semantic.md:78
msgid "These accessors return the reduced numerator (carrying the sign) and the positive denominator."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/semantic.md:104
msgid "Scalars"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/semantic.md:108
msgid "`SemanticScalar` is the meaning of one floating-point datum."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/semantic.md:118
msgid "`Rational` holds every finite value, including both signed zeros as $0/1$. `Infinity` carries `Negative` or `Positive`. `NaN` stands for every NaN: quiet or signalling, any payload, any sign. Equality is structural, so `NaN == NaN` is `true` here, unlike IEEE comparison."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/semantic.md:125
msgid "`from_bin_float(x)` returns the exact meaning of a `BinFloat`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/semantic.md:131
msgid "A finite $x = (-1)^{s} c\\, 2^{e}$ maps to `Rational` of `from_scaled_integer(±c, 2, e)`; an infinity maps to `Infinity(x.sign())`; a NaN maps to `NaN`. Precision is dropped. Never aborts."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/semantic.md:137
msgid "`from_decimal(x)` returns the exact meaning of an IEEE `@decimal.Decimal`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/semantic.md:143
msgid "A finite $x = (-1)^{s} c\\, 10^{q}$ maps to `Rational` of `from_scaled_integer(±c, 10, q)`, so every member of a cohort (`1.5`, `1.50`, `1.500`) maps to the same value $3/2$. Infinities and NaNs map as for binary. The GDA type `@decimal_gda.Decimal` has no projection in this package."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/semantic.md:179
msgid "Intervals"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/semantic.md:183
msgid "`SemanticInterval` is a closed interval given by two semantic endpoints."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/semantic.md:192
msgid "The fields are public and read-only outside the package. The type does not check that `lower` does not exceed `upper`; the projection below uses the reversed pair $(+\\infty, -\\infty)$ for the empty set."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/semantic.md:198
msgid "`from_ball_float(x)` projects the two endpoints of a `BallFloat`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/semantic.md:204
msgid "`lower` is `from_bin_float(x.lower_bound())` and `upper` is `from_bin_float(x.upper_bound())`. A bounded interval gives two `Rational` endpoints, an unbounded side gives `Infinity`, the entire line gives $(-\\infty, +\\infty)$, and the empty interval gives $(\\texttt{Infinity(Positive)}, \\texttt{Infinity(Negative)})$. Decorations, precision and flags are dropped."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/semantic.md:232
msgid "Errors and checked results"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/semantic.md:236
msgid "`SemanticError` is the representation-independent error vocabulary."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/semantic.md:250
msgid "It mirrors `ArithmeticErrorKind` of Luna-Flow/arithmetic without messages and without the certification detail."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/semantic.md:255
msgid "`from_arithmetic(err)` classifies an `ArithmeticError`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/semantic.md:261
msgid "The kind is tested in the order division by zero, parse, domain, format, unordered comparison, certification failure; anything else (the `UnsupportedOperation` kind) maps to `UnsupportedOperation`. The message and the `CertificationFailureDetail` are discarded."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/semantic.md:268
msgid "`SemanticResult[T]` is a semantic value or a semantic error."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/semantic.md:279
msgid "These functions turn a checked result of a concrete package into a semantic result, using a caller-supplied projection for the success case."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/semantic.md:294
msgid "and likewise for intervals. `f` is called at most once."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/semantic.md:318
msgid "Equality"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/semantic.md:320
msgid "Every type of the package derives `Eq`; the `equal` and `not_equal` methods are promoted explicitly. Prefer `==` and `!=`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/semantic.md:336
msgid "Equality of `ExactRational` is numeric equality because of the reduced form; equality of `SemanticScalar` adds `NaN == NaN` and distinguishes the two infinities. No ordering is provided."
msgstr ""

