#: manual/architecture.md:43
msgid "The separation prevents lossy conversions such as treating a GDA trap as an IEEE flag, an IEEE infinity as a generic error, or Empty, Entire and NaI as interchangeable interval failures. [Numeric semantics](./numeric_semantics.md) defines each of these states."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:48
msgid "Numeric core pipeline"
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:50
msgid "All scalar cores share one decomposition, even though their representations and standards differ:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:61
msgid "`BinFloat` stores a class, a sign, a non-negative binary coefficient with no trailing zero bits, an exponent, a precision, and NaN state (signaling bit and payload). `Decimal` and the GDA `Decimal` each store a sign, a package-owned base-$10^9$ coefficient, an exponent that is the quantum, a precision and the special state. `BallFloat` stores two `BinFloat` endpoints, a precision and an Empty marker; `BallFloatDecorated` adds a decoration without changing the bare representation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:69
msgid "Finalization is the semantic firewall. Kernels may compute exact sums, products, quotients, roots or guard and sticky bits, but only the finalizer decides the rounded value, the cohort, the flags, the traps, the decorations and the direction of interval endpoints. It also enforces the binary implementation exponent range $[1 - 2^{30},\\ 2^{30} - 1]$ for the leading bit: a result outside it is classified as an overflow or an underflow according to the rounding direction instead of being stored with a saturated exponent."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:77
msgid "Operations whose exact value is enormous never materialize it. A far addend collapses to a sticky bit, the IEEE remainder reduces a huge dividend modulo $2y$ by square-and-multiply on $2^k$, rounding to an integer splits the coefficient by shifting, and interval endpoint sums drop an addend that lies more than $\\max(65536, p)$ bits below the other to a directed sticky term. Each shortcut feeds the finalizer exactly the round and sticky information the full computation would."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:85
msgid "Algorithm selection"
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:87
msgid "Large-integer kernels use a staged selector rather than one algorithm:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:98
msgid "Division likewise moves from word division and Knuth's algorithm D to Burnikel–Ziegler and reciprocal Newton iteration where target measurements justify it. Sparse and unbalanced operands have their own paths, because an algorithm chosen only by the larger length can waste more on padding than it saves asymptotically."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:104
msgid "Crossover points are private, target-specific policy. They are measured with the Maremark benchmark hierarchy (`bench/*`) on dense, sparse, square, balanced and unbalanced data, and boundary tests compare exact results below, at and above every cutoff. Native, LLVM, Wasm, Wasm-GC and JavaScript may therefore pick different algorithms but must return the same public result."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:110
#: manual/design/bin_float.md:539
#: manual/design/decimal.md:571
msgid "Certified elementary functions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:112
msgid "Elementary functions (exponentials, logarithms, powers, roots, trigonometric, hyperbolic and inverse functions) follow one proof contract across the binary, decimal and interval stacks, in the style of Ziv's strategy:[^ziv]"
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:116
msgid "Decide results that are certainly outside the context range (overflow, underflow) from certified bounds on $\\log_2$ of the result, before any refinement."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:119
msgid "Compute directed lower and upper enclosures $[\\ell, h]$ of the exact value at a working precision $w = p + 64$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:121
msgid "Round both endpoints to the target. Because rounding is monotone, if $\\operatorname{rnd}(\\ell) = \\operatorname{rnd}(h)$ with the same flags, every value in $[\\ell, h]$, including the exact one, rounds to that result."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:124
msgid "Otherwise increase $w$ by $\\max(32, \\lfloor w/2 \\rfloor)$ and repeat, at most 12 times."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:126
msgid "If no attempt agrees, `try_*` functions return a `CertificationFailure` with the stage, reason, precision, working precision and attempt count; the non-`try` functions return a defined invalid result (a quiet NaN with *invalid operation* in binary) and never abort."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:131
msgid "`bin_float` owns the scalar dyadic certificates. `ball_float` lifts them over endpoints, critical points, poles and domain boundaries. `decimal` and `decimal_gda` convert exact decimal inputs to directed dyadic bounds, run the binary certificate, and convert the certified endpoints back through exact integer arithmetic; endpoints far outside the decimal range are replaced by representatives that round identically, so no huge decimal is expanded. Total interval functions may widen to a safe set such as $[-1, 1]$ or Entire, while their checked forms expose the failure. No path substitutes a host `Double` approximation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:141
msgid "A. Ziv, \"Fast evaluation of elementary mathematical functions with correctly rounded last bit\", *ACM TOMS* 17(3), 1991. Muller et al., *Handbook of Floating-Point Arithmetic*, 2nd ed., 2018, ch. 10, discusses the table-maker's dilemma this loop resolves."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:146
msgid "Decimal text conversion"
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:148
msgid "`BinFloat::from_string_ctx` and `to_decimal_string_ctx` are correctly rounded for every precision and exponent without expanding $10^{k}$ when $k$ is huge. Parsing first decides certain overflow or underflow from a logarithmic estimate; for small exponents it rounds the exact rational $D \\cdot 10^{k}$; for large ones it encloses $D \\cdot 10^{k}$ with directed powers of ten and widens the working precision until both ends round alike, which terminates because no tie is possible there. Formatting finds the leading decimal exponent from a binary estimate corrected by directed powers of ten, and `to_shortest_string_ctx` bisects on the digit count, which is valid because reading back is monotone in the number of digits."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:159
msgid "Context and status flow"
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:161
msgid "No numerical package relies on an ambient rounding mode."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:163
msgid "Binary and IEEE decimal contexts are immutable inputs; flags are explicit outputs that callers `combine`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:165
msgid "`decimal_gda` returns a new context whose status includes the raised flags, then selects at most one trap by fixed precedence."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:167
msgid "`BallContext` fixes the endpoint precision and exponent bounds and returns `BallFlags`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:169
msgid "`BinFloat`, `Decimal` and GDA `Decimal` implement the contextual traits of `Luna-Flow/arithmetic` (`AddContextual`, …, `ExpContextual`), so generic code can run all three under one `ArithmeticContext`; `BinaryContext::from_arithmetic_context` and the decimal equivalents carry its precision, rounding, exponent bounds and clamp."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:174
msgid "`BinFloatResult` and `BallFloatResult` keep the first `ArithmeticError`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:175
msgid "`DecimalChecked` keeps defined IEEE results, accumulates flags, and keeps certification errors separately."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:177
msgid "`GdaDecimalChecked` threads one outcome, stops on `Trapped`, and resumes only through the explicit `resume_defined` transition."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:180
msgid "These wrappers compose existing semantics; they add no arithmetic of their own and never merge incompatible status channels."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:183
msgid "Public surface and trait methods"
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:185
msgid "The generated `pkg.generated.mbti` of each package is the authority for what is public. With MoonBit 0.10, methods of a trait implementation are no longer promoted to the type automatically: a method such as `BinFloat::to_string`, `Decimal::add_contextual` or `BinFloat::sqrt_checked` is callable with dot syntax only because the package declares it with `pub extend`, and the `.mbti` then lists it as `pub fn Type::name`. A trait method that is not listed that way is still reachable through the trait (for example `@def.is_finite(x)` over `Floating`), but not as `x.method()`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:194
msgid "Parsing and execution"
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:196
msgid "`numeric_expr` holds syntax data and post-order callback evaluation. It performs no IO and selects no numeric backend."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:199
msgid "Each `frontend/*` package owns one external grammar:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:201
msgid "`gda_expr` parses `.decTest` directives and cases and executes GDA outcomes;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:202
msgid "`testfloat_expr` parses Berkeley TestFloat vectors and binds format, operation, rounding, tininess and exactness;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:204
msgid "`mpfr_expr` parses the pinned MPFR square-root, integer-power and elementary witnesses;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:206
msgid "`itl_expr` parses ITF1788 interval rows and classifies the declared support set."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:209
msgid "Frontends return typed summaries. The `cli` packages own files, filters, shards, rendering and exit codes, on top of `internal/conformance` (shards, case dispositions, summaries) and `internal/runner_cli` (options, files, JSON). Python tools under `tools/` fetch checksum-pinned data, plan tasks, run isolated targets and processes, and aggregate results; they never replace the MoonBit implementation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:216
msgid "Stability boundaries"
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:218
msgid "The application surface is `def`, the four numeric packages and the four checked wrappers. `semantic` and `numeric_expr` are provisional integration surfaces. Frontends are public so that repository runners can compose them, but their compatibility promise is limited to the declared corpora."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:223
msgid "`internal` and `internal/*`, `cli` and `cli/*`, `bench` and `bench/*`, `consistency` and `doc_examples` are implementation and verification infrastructure. A symbol may appear in `pkg.generated.mbti` without becoming a long-term application contract; read the package's design page before depending on it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:229
msgid "Invariants"
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:231
msgid "The sign of a value is independent of its non-negative coefficient."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:232
msgid "Finite binary normalization removes only factors of two."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:233
msgid "Decimal parsing preserves the quantum until a normalizing or reducing operation is called explicitly."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:235
msgid "Context finalization is the only place where a bounded result is rounded and status is decided."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:237
msgid "Interval lower endpoints round toward $-\\infty$ and upper endpoints toward $+\\infty$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:239
msgid "Empty, Entire, NaI, NaN, signed zero and the infinities stay explicit states."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:241
msgid "A finite result never carries an exponent outside the implementation range."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:242
msgid "Fast paths and fallbacks cannot change public values or status."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:243
msgid "Conformance summaries partition the selected cases, and sharding is deterministic."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:245
msgid "IO, downloads, process state and parallel scheduling stay in the tooling."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:247
msgid "Extension rule"
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:249
msgid "Add behaviour to the package that owns its semantics. Reuse the capability traits of `Luna-Flow/arithmetic` and `Luna-Flow/luna-generic` before creating an umbrella trait. Keep kernels private, contexts and status explicit, and external-format parsing outside the numeric types unless the format is a stable interchange contract. Promote a new trait method to dot syntax with `pub extend` when it belongs to the type's public surface."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:256
msgid "Extending a conformance surface needs coordinated changes to the parser, executor, support classification, CLI schema, corpus manifest, tests, generated interfaces and documentation. Parsing a new operation is not support until strict execution has a defined comparison and reproducible evidence; see [verification](./verification.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/ball_float.md:1
msgid "`ball_float` Conformance"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/ball_float.md:3
#: manual/performance_audit.md:154
msgid "Claim"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/ball_float.md:5
msgid "The current strict ITF1788 gate executes 4,656/4,656 selected cases with no failed, unsupported, or diagnostic rows. This is a claim about the pinned corpus revision, selected operation phases, and runner precision—not a claim of complete IEEE 1788 implementation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/ball_float.md:7
msgid "Semantic Oracle"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/ball_float.md:9
msgid "Expected results are compared by set meaning: endpoint containment, set relations, boolean relations, overlap states, numeric observations, and decorations use operation-specific comparators. A conservative interval may be valid when the contract permits widening; an enclosure that excludes an exact result is never valid."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/ball_float.md:11
msgid "Supported Phases"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/ball_float.md:13
msgid "The strict matrix covers sets, relations, observations, cancellation, add/subtract/multiply/divide, elementary core, exponential/logarithmic functions, general power, trigonometric functions, `atan2`, hyperbolic functions, inverse trigonometric functions, FMA, integer power, and extrema. Phase operation sets are disjoint so a row is never counted twice."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/ball_float.md:15
msgid "Decorations And Fallbacks"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/ball_float.md:17
