msgid "Alternatives rejected"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:688
msgid "**Midpoint–radius storage** (Arb-style): cheaper radii, but overestimating products and no representation for half-unbounded sets; see [above](#endpoints-not-midpoint-and-radius)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:691
msgid "**Hardware `Double` endpoints with switched rounding modes:** fixed precision, process-global state, and rounding-mode control is not available on every MoonBit target."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:694
msgid "**Round to nearest and inflate by one ulp** (\"epsilon inflation\"): simpler, but about one ulp wider per endpoint than directed rounding, and only valid for operations whose nearest-rounded result is known to be within one ulp, which excludes most elementary function kernels."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:698
msgid "**Aborting on uncertified elementary functions:** a single hard argument would stop a whole computation; the `try_` forms cover callers who need the failure."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:701
msgid "**Treating division by zero-containing intervals as an error:** IEEE 1788 defines these results as sets (Entire, half-unbounded or empty), and extended division is what makes interval Newton methods work."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:707
msgid "The package deliberately does not:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:709
msgid "provide the IEEE 1788 reverse operations (`sqrRev`, `mulRevToPair`, …) or two-output division;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:711
msgid "promise tight results: fallbacks and the dependency problem may widen enclosures arbitrarily;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:713
msgid "define a total order on intervals, or treat `Eq` as set equality;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:714
msgid "convert decimal data outward on its own: `from_double` and `exact` enclose binary values, and enclosing a decimal literal is the caller's job (see the [tutorial](../tutorial/ball_float.md#enclose-a-decimal-constant));"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:717
msgid "implement intervals over decimal endpoints, complex balls, interval vectors or matrices, or Taylor models;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:719
msgid "report status through global flags: flags come back from `*_ctx` calls and decorations travel with values."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:1
msgid "`ball_float_checked` design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:5
msgid "`ball_float_checked` lets an interval computation be written as one chain of operations in which construction mistakes and uncertifiable steps are explicit errors, while every successful value remains a rigorous enclosure. `BallFloatResult` closes `Result[BallFloat, ArithmeticError]` under the operations of [`ball_float`](ball_float.md) and adds no interval algorithm. The [API page](../api/ball_float_checked.md) lists the operations and the [tutorial](../tutorial/ball_float_checked.md) shows pipelines in use."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:15
#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:406
msgid "Enclosures"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:17
msgid "Write $\\mathbb{IR}$ for the set of closed intervals of the real line in the sense of IEEE 1788-2015 (set-based flavor): the empty set, bounded intervals $[\\ell, u]$, half-lines and $\\mathbb{R}$ itself.[^ieee1788] A `BallFloat` $X$ denotes an element of $\\mathbb{IR}$ whose finite endpoints are binary floats. An interval operation $F$ is an *enclosure* of a real function $f$ when"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:28
msgid "and likewise for binary operations. This is the fundamental theorem of interval arithmetic in the form `ball_float` implements: evaluating a formula with enclosures yields an enclosure of the formula's range, because enclosure is preserved by composition. If $F$ encloses $f$ and $G$ encloses $g$, then"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:39
msgid "using monotonicity of images under $\\subseteq$ for the first step and the enclosure property of $G$ for the second.[^moore]"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:42
msgid "IEEE 1788-2015, *Standard for Interval Arithmetic*, clauses 7–10."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:43
msgid "R. E. Moore, R. B. Kearfott, M. J. Cloud, *Introduction to Interval Analysis*, SIAM, 2009, chapter 5; van der Hoeven, \"Ball arithmetic\", 2009."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:46
msgid "The wrapper as an error monad"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:48
msgid "`BallFloatResult` is the exception monad $M V = V + E$ with $V$ the set of `BallFloat` values and $E$ the set of `ArithmeticError` values, with $\\eta = $ `ok` and $\\mathbin{>\\!\\!>\\!\\!=} = $ `bind`. The laws (left and right identity, associativity) and the functor laws of `map` are proved in the [`bin_float_checked` design](bin_float_checked.md#the-monad-laws); the proof is a case analysis on $\\mathrm{Ok}/\\mathrm{Err}$ that does not use any property of $V$. The operators are lifted by the same left-biased scheme (`ball_result_lift2`), so the [first-error theorem](bin_float_checked.md#the-first-error-theorem) applies unchanged: an expression reports the error of the first originating node in post-order."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:62
msgid "What is an error and what is a value"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:64
msgid "**Problem.** An interval computation can end in four kinds of situation: the input does not describe an interval; the operation has no meaning for an argument (degree-zero root); the library cannot certify a tight enough enclosure; or the true range is empty or unbounded."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:69
msgid "**Choice.** The first three are errors; the fourth is a value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:71
msgid "Situation"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:71
msgid "Outcome"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:73
msgid "NaN bound, $\\ell = +\\infty$, $u = -\\infty$, $\\ell > u$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:74
msgid "non-finite `exact`, `from_double`, `from_float` source"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:75
msgid "`rootn` of degree $0$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:76
msgid "enclosure not certifiable within budget"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:77
msgid "range empty ($\\ln$ of negatives, $1/\\{0\\}$)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:77
msgid "success: empty interval"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:77
#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:78
msgid "interval operation"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:78
msgid "range unbounded ($1/[-1,1]$)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:78
msgid "success: entire or half-line"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:80
msgid "**Why.** An empty or unbounded result is the *correct* enclosure of the true range, so turning it into an error would make the wrapper disagree with the set-based semantics: $\\ln([-1, 1]) = [-\\infty, 0]$ is a sound answer, and $\\ln(\\{-2\\}) = \\varnothing$ is the exact answer. By contrast, $[+\\infty, +\\infty]$ is not a set of reals at all, and a degree-zero root is undefined for every argument; these are mistakes of the caller. A certification failure means that the library refuses to return a result it cannot prove, which is also not an enclosure. Callers who consider an empty or unbounded result a failure in their application add that rule with `bind` (see the tutorial)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:90
msgid "Arithmetic operators cannot fail"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:92
msgid "`add`, `sub`, `mul` and `div` lift the `BallFloat` operators with `map`, so they never introduce an error: the four set operations always have an enclosure in $\\mathbb{IR}$ (for division, $X / Y$ is defined as the hull of $\\{ s/t : s \\in X, t \\in Y \\setminus \\{0\\} \\}$, empty when $Y = \\{0\\}$). The only errors in an arithmetic expression are those of its leaves."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:98
msgid "Powers go through the arithmetic traits"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:100
msgid "`pow_nat` and `pow_int` call `PowNatChecked::pow_nat_checked` and `PowIntChecked::pow_int_checked` of Luna-Flow/arithmetic with `ArithmeticContext::new(x.precision())`. `BallFloat` reads only the precision from the context (rounding direction and exponent bounds have no meaning for an outward-rounded enclosure), re-precisions the base, and computes the power of the whole interval. Using the power instead of repeated multiplication avoids the *dependency problem*: $X \\cdot X$ treats the two factors as independent, so for $X = [-1, 1]$ it gives $[-1, 1]$, whereas $\\{t^2 : t \\in X\\} = [0, 1]$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:109
msgid "No decorations and no flags"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:111
msgid "IEEE 1788 decorations (`com`, `dac`, `def`, `trv`, `ill`) record whether a function was defined and continuous on the input; `ball_float` provides them in its decorated type `BallFloatDecorated`. The wrapper does not carry them, because a decoration is information about a successful evaluation, not a failure, and combining it with the error channel would make every `map` responsible for decoration propagation. The same holds for `BallFlags`. Applications that need either use `ball_float` directly."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:119
msgid "Construction precision"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:121
msgid "The constructors use the defaults of `ball_float` (16 bits for integers, 53 for `Double`, 24 for `Float`, the source precision for `exact`, the larger bound precision for `from_bounds`). `from_bounds`, `exact`, `from_double` and `from_float` round outward, so the source value is always enclosed. `from_int` and `from_coefficient` first build a `BinFloat` with nearest rounding at $\\max(\\textit{precision}, 8)$ bits and then enclose that value; on the current branch this loses the integer when it needs more bits than the precision (see the warning in the API page)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:132
msgid "**Soundness.** If every leaf of an expression is a success enclosing the intended real input, and the expression evaluates to $\\mathrm{Ok}(Y)$, then $Y$ encloses the range of the real formula over the inputs. This is the composition argument above together with the fact that the wrapper applies exactly the `ball_float` operations on successes."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:137
msgid "**Error determinism.** If the expression evaluates to $\\mathrm{Err}(e)$, then $e$ is the error of the first originating node in post-order."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:139
msgid "**No hidden recovery.** No method turns an error into a value, and `map` never turns a value into an error ($\\texttt{map} = \\mathbin{>\\!\\!>\\!\\!=} \\circ\\, (\\eta \\circ -)$)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:142
msgid "**Cost.** Each wrapper step adds $O(1)$ work to the interval operation it delegates to."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:147
msgid "**Empty results as errors.** Breaks set-based semantics and would report an error for $\\ln([-1, 1])$ where a sound enclosure exists."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:149
msgid "**Carrying decorations in the wrapper.** Would duplicate the decorated API of `ball_float` with a second propagation rule."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:151
msgid "**Context variants (`exp_ctx`, …).** Enclosures are outward-rounded at the interval's precision; changing precision is done explicitly with `with_precision`, which encloses for every rounding mode."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:154
msgid "**Accumulating errors.** As for the binary wrapper, there is no combining operation on `ArithmeticError`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:159
msgid "No interval algorithm of its own; every enclosure comes from `ball_float`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:160
msgid "No decorations, `BallFlags`, interval contexts or interchange formats."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:161
msgid "No error recovery or accumulation; only the first error is kept."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:162
msgid "No `Eq`, `Show`, or enclosure relations on the wrapper itself; extract the `BallFloat` with `result()` to test containment or ordering."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float_checked.md:164
msgid "Out-of-domain parts of an argument are ignored, not reported."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:1
msgid "bench design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:5
msgid "Performance claims in `floating` must be reproducible and must not be confused with noise. `bench` turns the Maremark framework into a small, repository-specific toolkit: benchmarks are immutable specifications with a correctness oracle, every run records its environment and protocol, and timings are reduced with robust, paired statistics whose uncertainty is reported as a bootstrap confidence interval. Measurement (timing, streaming JSONL) happens in the per-core test packages and `tools/benchmark.py`; this package only describes experiments and reduces their data."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:16
msgid "What one observation measures"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:18
msgid "Maremark first *calibrates* a batch size for each implementation and dataset: starting from the protocol's minimum $n_0$ iterations, it times a batch and, while the batch is shorter than the target batch time $T$ (5 ms in the `Development` preset, 10 ms in `RegressionGate`) and the iteration cap is not reached, retries with"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:28
msgid "where $t_k$ is the measured batch time. An observation is then one timed batch of $n$ calls, recorded as the mean per-call time $x = t_{\\text{batch}} / n$ in microseconds. Averaging inside a batch removes clock granularity; the batch-to-batch variation that remains is what the statistics below handle."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:34
msgid "Blocks and pairing"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:36
msgid "The confirmatory phase consists of $m$ blocks ($m = 10$ for `Development`, $20$ for `RegressionGate`). In block $j$ every implementation is measured once, in the cyclic order $(j + o) \\bmod K$ of the $K$ implementations, with an offset $o$ derived from the seed, so each implementation runs first equally often over $K$ consecutive blocks. For a baseline $B$ and a candidate $C$ the observations are sorted by block and paired:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:47
msgid "Slow drifts (frequency scaling, thermal state, background load) affect $b_j$ and $c_j$ of the same block similarly and cancel in $d_j$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:50
msgid "Point estimates"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:52
msgid "With $\\operatorname{med}$ the sample median,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:60
msgid "The median has a breakdown point of 50 %: up to half of the blocks can be arbitrarily disturbed (a preempted batch, a page-fault storm) without moving the estimate arbitrarily. This is the outlier handling of the `bench` reductions: no observation is discarded. Maremark's protocols also name an outlier policy (`ReportOnly` for `Development`, `TukeyFence` for `RegressionGate`); it is part of the recorded protocol identity, but the observations are emitted unfiltered and the reductions in this package do not apply a filter."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:69
msgid "The decision compares $\\Delta_{\\%}$ with a practical threshold $\\delta$: `Faster` if $\\Delta_{\\%} \\le -\\delta$, `Slower` if $\\Delta_{\\%} \\ge \\delta$, `Equivalent` otherwise. A difference smaller than $\\delta$ is treated as irrelevant however precisely it is measured."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:74
msgid "Sample quantiles"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:76
msgid "Maremark uses the linear-interpolation quantile (type 7 of Hyndman and Fan[^hf]): for sorted values $s_0 \\le \\dots \\le s_{N-1}$ and $f \\in [0, 1]$, with $h = f (N - 1)$,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:85
msgid "so $Q(1/2)$ is the usual median (the mean of the two middle values for even $N$)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:88
msgid "R. J. Hyndman, Y. Fan, \"Sample quantiles in statistical packages\", *The American Statistician* 50(4), 1996."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:91
msgid "The bootstrap confidence interval"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:93
msgid "The uncertainty of $\\operatorname{med}(d)$ is estimated with the percentile bootstrap[^efron]. For $r = 1, \\dots, R$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:96
msgid "draw $m$ indices $i_1, \\dots, i_m$ uniformly with replacement from $\\{1, \\dots, m\\}$;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:98
msgid "compute the resampled median $\\theta^{*}_r = \\operatorname{med}(d_{i_1}, \\dots, d_{i_m})$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:101
msgid "Sort $\\theta^{*}_{(1)} \\le \\dots \\le \\theta^{*}_{(R)}$. For a confidence level $c$ (in percent) let $\\alpha = (100 - c) / 200$. The interval is"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:108
msgid "with $Q^{*}$ the type-7 quantile of the sorted bootstrap medians. With $c = 95$ and $R = 10\\,000$ this is $[Q^{*}(0.025), Q^{*}(0.975)]$, i.e. linear interpolation at positions $249.975$ and $9749.025$ of the sorted resamples (0-based). The interval is on $\\operatorname{med}(d)$ in microseconds (not in percent)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:114
msgid "The random indices come from a xorshift64\\* generator[^vigna] seeded with the caller's seed (a zero seed is replaced by a fixed constant), and an index is the state modulo $m$; the modulo bias is at most $m / 2^{64}$. With a fixed seed the interval is a deterministic function of the data, so reruns of an analysis reproduce it exactly."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:120
msgid "B. Efron, R. J. Tibshirani, *An Introduction to the Bootstrap*, Chapman & Hall, 1993, chapter 13."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:123
msgid "S. Vigna, \"An experimental exploration of Marsaglia's xorshift generators, scrambled\", *ACM TOMS* 42(4), 2016."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:126
msgid "The regression verdict"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:128
msgid "`is_significant_regression` requires both"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:134
msgid "the median slowdown must be practically relevant *and* the 95 % interval of the median paired difference must exclude zero on the slow side. The first condition guards against flagging tiny but precisely measured changes, the second against flagging large but noisy ones."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:141
msgid "Paired medians instead of means"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:143
msgid "Timing distributions are right-skewed with occasional large outliers. The mean of differences and a $t$ interval would be dominated by those outliers; the median of block-paired differences is robust and needs no normality assumption, and the bootstrap gives its interval without a variance formula for the median."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:149
msgid "Fixed parameters per use"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:151
msgid "`confirmatory_regression` fixes $\\delta = 3\\,\\%$, $R = 10\\,000$ and $c = 95\\,\\%$, so a regression gate cannot be weakened by a caller. `paired_hotspot` takes $\\delta$ and the seed from the caller and uses $R = 2000$ and $c = 95\\,\\%$ for exploratory hotspot reports."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:156
msgid "Auto-tuning by minimum median"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:158
msgid "`tune_dataset` scores each candidate by the median of its valid confirmatory samples and returns the one with the smallest median, breaking exact ties by candidate id. The score is passed to `@tune.select_best` as both primary and secondary criterion, so among the candidates within $\\delta$ of the fastest the smallest secondary, which is again the fastest, wins; the practical threshold therefore has no effect on the choice. The crossover between two candidates across scales is computed by Maremark from the per-dataset labels."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:166
msgid "Immutable fixtures with an oracle"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:168
msgid "`immutable_bench` generates each input once per scale and checks every output against an independent reference. A faster implementation that computes the wrong result fails the run instead of winning a comparison."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:172
msgid "Environment as data"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:174
msgid "`environment` records the target, profile and a data-type label with every run, and marks what only the outer tool knows (machine, frequency policy, commit) as `external-metadata`. A reader of two artifacts can then tell whether their numbers are comparable; the package itself does not compare runs across environments."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:182
msgid "**Determinism of the reduction.** For fixed observations and seed, `paired_hotspot`, `confirmatory_regression` and `tune_dataset` return the same values on every run (sorting by block id, seeded generator, deterministic tie-breaking)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:186
msgid "**Pairing.** Samples are paired by block order. Unequal sample counts are an error (`MismatchedPairs`), never a silent truncation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:188
msgid "**Interval ordering.** $L \\le U$, because $Q^{*}$ is monotone in its argument and $\\alpha < 1 - \\alpha$ for $c > 0$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:190
msgid "**Scale invariance.** Multiplying all samples by a constant $\\lambda > 0$ multiplies $\\operatorname{med}(d)$, $L$ and $U$ by $\\lambda$ and leaves $\\Delta_{\\%}$, the decision and the verdict unchanged, so the unit of time does not matter."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:197
msgid "**Discarding outliers before averaging.** Any fence needs a tuning constant and silently changes the sample; the median makes it unnecessary."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:199
msgid "**Unpaired comparison of two sample sets.** Drift between the measurement of $B$ and $C$ would enter the difference directly."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:201
msgid "**Normal-theory intervals.** They assume symmetric errors that timing data does not have."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:206
msgid "No timing, process control or file output: Maremark's runner and `tools/benchmark.py` do that."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:208
msgid "No absolute performance targets; thresholds apply to relative differences."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench.md:209
msgid "Performance results never change correctness claims, and the benchmarks run only on the native target."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/ball_float.md:1
msgid "bench/ball_float design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/ball_float.md:5
msgid "Measure the cost of each layer of the interval stack separately, on inputs where all layers must produce the same result, so that a change in one layer shows up as a change in one reported percentage."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/ball_float.md:11
#: manual/design/bench/bin_float.md:11
#: manual/design/bench/decimal.md:11
#: manual/design/bench/decimal_gda.md:11
msgid "For one dataset let $k_j$, $c_j$ and $f_j$ be the per-call times of the kernel, core and checked paths in block $j$ (a case without a kernel path reports only the second quantity). The reported quantities are"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/ball_float.md:21
#: manual/design/bench/bin_float.md:21
#: manual/design/bench/decimal.md:21
#: manual/design/bench/decimal_gda.md:21
msgid "the relative median paired overheads of one layer over the layer below. The estimators, the pairing and the bootstrap interval are derived in the [bench design](../bench.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/ball_float.md:27
#: manual/design/bench/bin_float.md:27
#: manual/design/bench/decimal.md:27
#: manual/design/bench/decimal_gda.md:27
msgid "Workloads"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/ball_float.md:33
msgid "Inputs are exact singleton intervals built from fixed bit patterns; the precision is the sum of the operand bit lengths plus 4 and the `div` dividend is the product of the patterns, so every result is exact and every interval result must stay a singleton."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/ball_float.md:35
#: manual/design/bench/bin_float.md:37
#: manual/design/bench/decimal.md:35
#: manual/design/bench/decimal_gda.md:35
msgid "Correctness oracle"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/ball_float.md:37
msgid "Kernel outputs must equal the exact `BinFloat` result; interval outputs must be singletons whose center equals it. An output that disagrees with the oracle is counted as a failure and the performance test asserts that there is none, so a comparison is never between paths that compute different things."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/ball_float.md:41
#: manual/design/bench/bin_float.md:43
#: manual/design/bench/decimal.md:41
#: manual/design/bench/decimal_gda.md:41
msgid "Exact inputs"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/ball_float.md:43
#: manual/design/bench/bin_float.md:45
#: manual/design/bench/decimal.md:43
#: manual/design/bench/decimal_gda.md:43
msgid "Exact results make the arithmetic work identical across paths: no path can win by rounding earlier, and the measured difference is the cost of representation, context handling and checking."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/ball_float.md:49
#: manual/design/bench/bin_float.md:51
#: manual/design/bench/decimal.md:49
#: manual/design/bench/decimal_gda.md:49
msgid "Plan tests compile every specification in ordinary test runs, so the benchmarks cannot rot unnoticed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/ball_float.md:51
#: manual/design/bench/bin_float.md:53
#: manual/design/bench/decimal.md:51
#: manual/design/bench/decimal_gda.md:51
msgid "Performance tests assert `failed_count == 0`: every output matched its oracle."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/ball_float.md:53
#: manual/design/bench/bin_float.md:55
#: manual/design/bench/decimal.md:53
#: manual/design/bench/decimal_gda.md:53
msgid "Seeds are fixed (`20260715`), so the measurement order and the bootstrap resamples are reproducible."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/ball_float.md:58
#: manual/design/bench/bin_float.md:60
#: manual/design/bench/decimal.md:58
#: manual/design/bench/decimal_gda.md:58
msgid "**Random inputs per sample.** They would mix input variance into timing variance; fixed patterns per dataset isolate the cost of the code."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/ball_float.md:60
#: manual/design/bench/bin_float.md:62
#: manual/design/bench/decimal.md:60
#: manual/design/bench/decimal_gda.md:60
msgid "**Only end-to-end timings.** A single number cannot tell whether a regression is in the kernel, the core or the checked wrapper."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/ball_float.md:65
#: manual/design/bench/bin_float.md:67
#: manual/design/bench/decimal.md:65
#: manual/design/bench/decimal_gda.md:65
msgid "Native target only; no claims for other targets."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/ball_float.md:66
#: manual/design/bench/bin_float.md:68
#: manual/design/bench/decimal.md:66
#: manual/design/bench/decimal_gda.md:66
msgid "No public API; the package exists for `tools/benchmark.py`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/bench/ball_float.md:67
#: manual/design/bench/bin_float.md:69
#: manual/design/bench/decimal.md:67
#: manual/design/bench/decimal_gda.md:67
