msgid "Imports name packages, not files: each directory with a `moon.pkg` is one package, and its files share one namespace. By convention the manual imports `Luna-Flow/arithmetic` as `@lf_arith` and `Luna-Flow/luna-generic` as `@lf_alg`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:65
msgid "Build values"
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:67
msgid "A `BinFloat` is an exact dyadic number $c \\cdot 2^e$ with a precision; a `Decimal` is $c \\cdot 10^q$ and remembers the quantum $q$ of its literal; a `BallFloat` is an interval with binary endpoints."
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:95
msgid "`BinFloat` prints in the exact form `<coefficient>p<exponent>`; use `to_shortest_string` or `to_decimal_string_ctx` for decimal text. Call `normalized()` on a decimal only when you want to drop its cohort."
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:99
msgid "Other constructors: `BinFloat::from_int`, `from_double`, `from_string` and `from_string_ctx` (correctly rounded decimal parsing), `from_hex`; `Decimal::from_int`, `from_string` and `from_string_ctx`; `BallFloat::from_int`, `from_double`, `exact` and `from_bounds`. Constructors take an optional `precision`; `BallFloat::from_int` defaults to 16 bits, so pass `precision=53` when you want binary64-like endpoints."
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:106
msgid "Choose a context"
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:108
msgid "The plain operators (`+`, `-`, `*`, `/` and `add`, `mul`, …) work at the operands' precision with round-to-nearest-even and an effectively unbounded exponent range, and they discard status. When precision, exponent range, rounding direction, tininess or the status flags are part of your result, use the `*_ctx` form with an explicit context:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:141
msgid "Contexts are immutable values; nothing in the library reads a global rounding mode. IEEE contexts return the flags of one operation, which you `combine` yourself. A GDA context instead travels with the result: every `GdaOutcome` returns the next context, whose status accumulates the flags."
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:146
msgid "Choose a failure model"
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:148
msgid "The library exposes several failure channels on purpose. Pick the one that matches what your caller must observe:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:151
msgid "`Option` from simple constructors such as `Decimal::from_string`, when bad input needs no diagnostic."
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:153
msgid "`Result[T, ArithmeticError]` from checked operations (`div_checked`, `sqrt`, `compare_checked`, `from_string`, the `try_*_ctx` elementary functions)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:156
msgid "`BinaryFlags`, `DecimalFlags` and `BallFlags` beside a defined result."
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:157
msgid "`GdaOutcome[T]`, which keeps the GDA-defined result even when a trap fires."
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:158
msgid "The pipeline wrappers: `BinFloatResult` and `BallFloatResult` stop at the first error; `DecimalChecked` keeps defined NaN and infinity results and accumulates flags; `GdaDecimalChecked` stops at a trap and keeps its outcome."
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:162
msgid "Empty, Entire and NaI, which are interval values, not errors."
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:182
msgid "Do not fold these channels into one exception type or one `Result`: that erases semantics the standards make observable."
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:185
msgid "Read results correctly"
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:187
msgid "Signed zero, infinities, quiet and signaling NaN, and NaN payloads are observable on scalars."
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:189
msgid "`compare`, `<` and sorting use a total preorder in which every NaN is equal to every other NaN and above every number, and $-0 = +0$. For IEEE semantics use `compare_checked`, the quiet and signaling predicates of `bin_float`, or the `total_order*` functions."
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:193
msgid "`==` on `BinFloat` and `BallFloat` compares representations (sign of zero and precision included); `==` on `Decimal` compares values. Use `compare(a, b) == 0` for numeric equality of binary values."
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:196
msgid "A `BallFloat` has containment and set relations (`contains`, `subset`, `definitely_lt`, …), not a scalar order. A result is correct when it encloses the exact result; tightness is a separate quality."
msgstr ""

#: manual/getting_started.md:199
msgid "`SemanticScalar` compares mathematical values across packages and drops precision, quantum, signed zero, payloads, decorations and flags."
msgstr ""

