#: manual/tutorial/ball_float.md:74
msgid "Build intervals"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/ball_float.md:76
msgid "Choose the constructor by what you know about the value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/ball_float.md:100
msgid "`from_bounds` and `exact` abort on invalid input (a NaN endpoint, reversed bounds, a non-finite point); their `try_` forms return the same condition as an `ArithmeticError`. `new(center, radius)` is a constructor view: the stored value is always the endpoint pair, and `center()` / `radius()` recompute the midpoint–radius form from it. The whole real line and the empty set are `BallFloat::whole()` and `BallFloat::empty()`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/ball_float.md:107
msgid "Enclose a decimal constant"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/ball_float.md:109
msgid "`BallFloat::from_double(0.1)` encloses the binary64 number nearest to $0.1$, which is not $0.1$. To enclose the decimal value itself, round the decimal string once down and once up, and use the two results as bounds."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/ball_float.md:136
msgid "Do this at every boundary where data enters from decimal text or from a rounded computation; inside the interval domain the guarantee is then kept automatically."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/ball_float.md:140
msgid "Compute and read the result"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/ball_float.md:142
msgid "The arithmetic operators return an interval that contains every result of the operation applied to points of the operands."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/ball_float.md:166
msgid "Read the result with `lower_bound()` / `upper_bound()` for the endpoints, `width()` for $\\overline{x} - \\underline{x}$ rounded up, `midpoint()` for a representative point, and `is_bounded()` / `is_entire()` / `is_empty()` for the shape."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/ball_float.md:171
msgid "Compare intervals"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/ball_float.md:173
msgid "An interval stands for an unknown point inside it, so \"is $x < y$?\" has three answers: certainly, possibly, or certainly not. The relations say which."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/ball_float.md:200
msgid "`contains` tests a point; `subset`, `interior` and `set_equal` compare sets; `definitely_lt`, `definitely_le` and `definitely_gt` hold only when the order holds for every pair of points; `maybe_eq` (the same as `overlaps`) holds when some pair may be equal; `overlap_state` gives the full IEEE 1788 classification of two intervals."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/ball_float.md:206
msgid "Evaluate elementary functions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/ball_float.md:208
msgid "Functions are evaluated so that the result contains $f(\\xi)$ for every $\\xi$ in the argument where $f$ is defined. Extrema inside the interval and poles are taken into account."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/ball_float.md:236
msgid "Every `*_interval` function has a total form, which always returns a valid enclosure, and most also have a `try_*_interval` form. The two differ only when the certified evaluation runs out of its precision budget: the total form then widens to a safe range (for example $[-1, 1]$ for `sin_interval`), and the `try_` form returns an `ArithmeticError` describing the failure."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/ball_float.md:242
msgid "Round to a target format"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/ball_float.md:244
msgid "`BallContext` describes a binary format (precision and exponent range). `apply_ctx` rounds an interval outward into it and reports what happened in `BallFlags`; the `*_ctx` operators compute and then apply the context."
msgstr ""

