msgid "Going further"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/ball_float.md:272
msgid "`BallFloatDecorated` pairs an interval with an IEEE 1788 decoration that records what is known about the function evaluation that produced it: `com` (defined, continuous and bounded), `dac` (defined and continuous), `def` (defined), `trv` (nothing known) and `ill` (the special value NaI, \"not an interval\")."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/ball_float.md:302
msgid "Use decorations when a caller must learn whether a result is backed by a function that was defined and continuous on the whole input — the condition under which interval results prove existence theorems such as Brouwer's fixed point theorem. When only the enclosure matters, bare `BallFloat` is simpler."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/ball_float.md:307
msgid "Generic code over the enclosure traits"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/ball_float.md:309
msgid "`BallFloat` implements the enclosure relations of [`Luna-Flow/arithmetic`](https://lunaflow.cn/en/arithmetic/) (`Contains`, `Overlaps`, `DefinitelyLt`, `DefinitelyLe`, `MaybeEq`), so code written against those traits works for any enclosure type."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/ball_float.md:338
msgid "The checked capabilities `DivChecked`, `PowNatChecked` and `PowIntChecked` take an `@lf_arith.ArithmeticContext`; its `precision` sets the result precision. They never fail for `BallFloat`: division by an interval containing zero returns an unbounded enclosure."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/ball_float.md:343
msgid "Checked pipelines"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/ball_float.md:345
msgid "When a computation has several steps that may fail (invalid construction, uncertified elementary functions), the companion package [`ball_float_checked`](ball_float_checked.md) threads the first error through a chain of operations so that you test for failure once at the end."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/ball_float.md:350
msgid "Precision and cost"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/ball_float.md:352
msgid "Each interval carries a working `precision` in bits; results of binary operations use the larger precision of the two operands. Arithmetic costs a few endpoint operations at that precision. Elementary functions evaluate certified series at about $p + 64$ to $p + 192$ bits and may refine up to 12 times on hard inputs, so they cost several times more than one multiplication. Raising the precision narrows the rounding part of the width, but not the part that comes from the width of the inputs."
msgstr ""

