#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:3
msgid "This tutorial teaches you to compute with `BinFloat`, the arbitrary-precision binary floating-point type of `floating`: build exact values, run a calculation in an IEEE 754 format such as binary32 with its status flags, read and write interchange bits and decimal text, and call correctly rounded elementary functions. Every example compiles and shows its output. The mathematics behind the rounding rules is on the [design page](../design/bin_float.md), and every function is listed in the [API reference](../api/bin_float.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:14
msgid "Add the module and import the package in your `moon.pkg`:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:26
msgid "The smallest useful program divides one by three, once with 53 bits and once in the binary32 format, and prints both results as the shortest decimal that reads back exactly:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:43
msgid "The operator `/` rounds to nearest-even at the precision of its operands (53 bits by default). `div_ctx` rounds under the binary32 context and also returns the IEEE flags; here `inexact` says that one third is not a binary number."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:49
msgid "Build exact values"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:51
msgid "A finite `BinFloat` is $c \\cdot 2^{e}$ with an integer coefficient $c$ and an exponent $e$. `make` takes both plus a precision; `to_string` prints the stored form `c p e`, which is exact."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:71
msgid "Read decimal input with `from_string`, which rounds the decimal value itself. Converting through a host `Double` rounds twice when you want a precision other than 53 bits, and `from_double` faithfully keeps the host's binary approximation:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:90
msgid "Compute in an IEEE format and keep the flags"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:92
msgid "A `BinaryContext` fixes the precision, exponent range, rounding direction and tininess rule of an operation. The `*_ctx` methods return the value and the flags of that one operation; combine flags yourself to accumulate them."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:115
msgid "$120000$ exceeds the largest binary16 value $65504$, so the sum overflows to infinity. The product $10^{-7}$ lies below the smallest normal binary16 number $2^{-14} \\approx 6.1 \\cdot 10^{-5}$, so it is rounded on the subnormal grid and raises underflow. The rounding direction changes the overflow result: toward zero it stays at the largest finite value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:135
msgid "Read and write interchange bits"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:137
msgid "Use `BinaryInterchange` when a file or protocol carries binary16, binary32, binary64 or binary128 bit patterns. It never goes through a host `Double`, so signaling NaNs, payloads and binary128 survive."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:156
msgid "Encoding rounds into the format, so it returns flags as well. Decoding is always exact."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:159
msgid "Print decimal text"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:161
msgid "`to_shortest_string_ctx` gives the shortest decimal that reads back as the same value in a format; for binary64 it matches the host formatter. `to_decimal_string_ctx` gives a fixed number of significant digits, rounded in the context's direction, and reports whether digits were dropped."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:183
msgid "Every binary number has a finite decimal expansion, so 55 digits print the binary64 value of `0.1` exactly."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:186
msgid "Call elementary functions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:188
msgid "Each elementary function has a plain form, a `*_ctx` form and a `try_*_ctx` form. All three return the correctly rounded result. They differ only in how a domain error or an exhausted refinement budget is reported: the plain and `*_ctx` forms return a quiet NaN with `invalid_operation`, while `try_*_ctx` returns an `@lf_arith.ArithmeticError` that says what went wrong."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:213
msgid "`sinpi(0.5)` is exactly one, and the function knows it: exact results raise no `inexact` flag."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:218
msgid "Enclose a real number with directed rounding"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:220
msgid "Rounding toward negative and toward positive infinity brackets the exact result. This is how you build a guaranteed enclosure without an interval library:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:244
msgid "`ball_float` packages this idea as midpoint–radius arithmetic; see the [ball_float tutorial](ball_float.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:247
msgid "Use fused and exact IEEE operations"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:249
msgid "`fma` rounds $x y + z$ once, so it recovers the rounding error of a product. `remainder` is always exact for operands of one precision."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:267
msgid "The two-product error term is the basis of compensated algorithms (Kahan summation, double-double arithmetic); the design page shows why it is exact."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:270
msgid "Watch underflow and tininess"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:272
msgid "IEEE 754 lets an implementation detect tininess before or after rounding, and the choice changes the underflow flag for values just below the smallest normal number. `BinaryContext` lets you choose:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:297
msgid "The value is the same; only the flag differs. Hardware differs too: x86 detects tininess after rounding and ARM before rounding, and TestFloat checks both rules."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:301
msgid "Write generic code"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:303
msgid "`BinFloat` implements the `@def.Floating` trait shared by all scalar cores of `floating`, and the checked and contextual traits of `@lf_arith`. Code written against those traits runs on `BinFloat`, `Decimal` and the other cores:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:334
msgid "The contextual traits turn the IEEE `division_by_zero` and `invalid_operation` flags into errors and report the other flags as diagnostics. For a whole pipeline that stops at the first error, use [`bin_float_checked`](bin_float_checked.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:339
msgid "Performance"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:341
msgid "Precision costs time roughly like integer multiplication of that many bits. The coefficient kernel switches automatically from schoolbook to Karatsuba, Toom-3 and number-theoretic transforms, so thousands of bits are practical. Elementary functions refine their working precision until the result is certain, and the first attempt succeeds for almost every input. Pick one precision for an algorithm instead of widening and narrowing repeatedly. The [performance page](../performance/bin_float.md) records measurements."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:351
msgid ""
"**`==` compares representations.** `BinFloat` derives `Eq`, so `1` at 53 bits is not `==` to `1` at 24 bits, $-0 \\n"
"e +0$, and a NaN equals an identical NaN. Use `compare(x, y) == 0` for numerical equality, or `equal_quiet` for the IEEE predicate."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:355
msgid "**`compare` puts NaN last.** It never aborts: every NaN compares equal to every NaN and greater than every number, so `nan > x` is true. Use `compare_checked`, `compare_quiet` or `less_quiet` when NaN must be unordered."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:359
msgid "**Plain operators ignore your format.** `x + y` works at the larger operand precision with an exponent range of about $2^{\\pm 2^{30}}$; it never overflows at binary32 limits. Use `add_ctx` with a format context."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:362
msgid "**`from_double(0.1)` is not one tenth.** It is the binary64 value the host already rounded. Parse decimal text with `from_string`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:364
msgid "**`to_string` is not decimal.** It prints `3602879701896397p-55`. Use `to_shortest_string` or `to_decimal_string_ctx` for people."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:366
msgid "**`from_hex` has no hexadecimal point.** `0x3p-1` means $3 \\cdot 2^{-1}$; `0x1.8p0` is rejected."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:368
msgid "**`with_precision` hides flags.** Use `round_ctx` when you need to know that narrowing was inexact or overflowed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:370
msgid "**Known defects.** On the current branch `acos` recurses without end on a NaN, an infinity or $|x| > 1$, `atan2` mishandles signed zeros in two IEEE special cases, and `pow` rejects a negative base with an integral exponent of magnitude at least $2^{31}$ and a base of $-0$ with most non-integral exponents, and flags exact results such as $16^{3/4} = 8$ as inexact. The [API reference](../api/bin_float.md#binfloatasin-binfloatacos-binfloatatan-binfloatatan2) lists the details."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:379
msgid "[bin_float design](../design/bin_float.md): the error model, how correct rounding is decided, overflow and tininess, certified elementary functions."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:381
msgid "[bin_float API](../api/bin_float.md): every item with its special values."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:382
msgid "[bin_float conformance](../conformance/bin_float.md): the TestFloat and MPFR evidence."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:384
msgid "[bin_float_checked tutorial](bin_float_checked.md) and [ball_float tutorial](ball_float.md): pipelines and enclosures built on `BinFloat`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float.md:387
msgid "[Luna-Flow arithmetic](https://lunaflow.cn/en/arithmetic/): the checked and contextual traits."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float_checked.md:1
msgid "`bin_float_checked` tutorial"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float_checked.md:3
msgid "This tutorial shows how to write a binary floating-point computation in which every step that can fail is checked, without unwrapping a `Result` after each step: you wrap the inputs in `BinFloatResult`, chain operations and operators, add your own validation with `bind`, and inspect the first error once at the end. The wrapper delegates all arithmetic to [`bin_float`](bin_float.md). The formal model is in the [design page](../design/bin_float_checked.md); every method is listed in the [API reference](../api/bin_float_checked.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float_checked.md:24
msgid "Compute $\\sqrt{81} / 3$ at 48 bits and read the result:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float_checked.md:39
msgid "`to_string` prints the exact binary value: `3p0` is $3 \\cdot 2^0$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float_checked.md:43
msgid "The remaining examples print results with this helper:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float_checked.md:55
msgid "Write formulas with operators"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float_checked.md:57
msgid "`+`, `-`, `*`, `/` and unary `-` work on wrappers. The first failing step decides the result; later steps do not run on an error:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float_checked.md:79
msgid "On plain `BinFloat` values the second call would silently produce a number ($1/0 = \\infty$ and $2/\\infty = 0$); the wrapper reports where the computation left the real numbers."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float_checked.md:83
msgid "Add your own checks with `bind`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float_checked.md:85
msgid "`map` applies a function that cannot fail; `bind` applies one that returns a `BinFloatResult` and may fail. Use `bind` to put an application rule into the pipeline:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float_checked.md:114
msgid "Control precision and rounding with a context"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float_checked.md:116
msgid "Every elementary function has a `_ctx` form that takes a `BinaryContext`. The plain form rounds to nearest at the operand's own precision; the context form rounds to the context:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float_checked.md:135
msgid "Tell domain errors from certification failures"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float_checked.md:137
msgid "Errors are `ArithmeticError` values from Luna-Flow/arithmetic. Their kind says whether the input was outside the function's domain or the library could not certify a correctly rounded result:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float_checked.md:163
msgid "`exp_ln` (the fused $\\ln(\\exp x)$) is certified only for $|x| \\le 1/8$, so it fails for $3$; this is a certification limit, not a mathematical domain."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float_checked.md:168
msgid "Generic code over Luna-Flow/arithmetic traits"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float_checked.md:170
msgid "`BinFloat` implements the checked traits of Luna-Flow/arithmetic (`SqrtChecked`, `DivChecked`, `PowIntChecked`, `PowNatChecked`). A function written against those traits returns `Result[T, ArithmeticError]`, and `from_result` brings it into a pipeline:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float_checked.md:202
msgid "Keep IEEE flags when they matter"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float_checked.md:204
msgid "The wrapper stores a value or an error, nothing else. When the inexact, overflow or underflow flags are part of your contract, call the `BinFloat::*_ctx` operations, which return `(value, BinaryFlags)`, and combine the flags yourself:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float_checked.md:226
msgid "**NaN is a success.** `BinFloatResult` reports what `bin_float` reports as an error. Operations that IEEE defines with a NaN or infinite result ($\\infty - \\infty$, `sqrt_ctx` of a negative number, `div_ctx` by zero) are successful values. Test `@def.is_nan` on the final value if that matters."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float_checked.md:230
msgid "**`div` and `div_ctx` differ.** `div` (and `/`) fails on a zero divisor; `div_ctx` returns an infinity. The same holds for `pow_int` versus `pow_int_ctx` and `sqrt` versus `sqrt_ctx`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float_checked.md:233
msgid "**Only the first error survives.** In `a + b` with both operands failing, the error of `a` is reported. Errors are not collected."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float_checked.md:235
msgid "**The `_ctx` methods drop flags.** Use `bin_float` directly for flags."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float_checked.md:236
msgid "**Precision of mixed operands.** Binary operations use the larger operand precision; `from_float` defaults to 24 bits, so mixing it with `from_double` values gives 53-bit results."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float_checked.md:243
msgid "[`bin_float_checked` design](../design/bin_float_checked.md): the error monad, its laws and the first-error theorem."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float_checked.md:245
msgid "[`bin_float_checked` API](../api/bin_float_checked.md): every method."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float_checked.md:246
msgid "[`bin_float` tutorial](bin_float.md): contexts, flags and interchange formats."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/bin_float_checked.md:247
msgid "[`ball_float_checked` tutorial](ball_float_checked.md): the same pipeline for enclosures."
msgstr ""

