#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:1
msgid "decimal tutorial"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:3
msgid "This tutorial shows how to compute with decimal numbers that behave the way people write them: `0.1 + 0.2` is exactly `0.3`, `12.30` remembers that it has two decimal places, and every rounding is chosen by you and reported back to you. You will parse and format values, compute under a context and read its flags, round money with `quantize`, exchange decimal64 bits, and call correctly rounded elementary functions. The mathematics behind each step is in the [decimal design](../design/decimal.md); every function is specified in the [decimal API](../api/decimal.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:14
msgid "Add `floating` to your module and import the package:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:26
msgid "The smallest useful program adds two decimal fractions that a `Double` cannot represent:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:39
msgid "`0.1` is $1/10$, and no power of two is divisible by 5, so binary floating point can only approximate it; decimal floating point stores it exactly."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:44
msgid "Parse amounts and keep their scale"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:46
msgid "Parsing keeps the exponent of the text. Two values can be equal and still carry different information:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:62
msgid "`12.30` and `12.3` are two members of the same *cohort*: numerically equal, written with different exponents. `normalized()` picks the shortest member. Do not call it on amounts whose number of decimal places matters; call it when you want a canonical key."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:67
msgid "Integers are built reduced: `Decimal::from_int(1000)` is stored as $1 \\times 10^{3}$ and prints as `1E+3`. Parse `\"1000\"` when you want the four digits."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:70
msgid "Compute under a context and keep the flags"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:72
msgid "A `DecimalContext` fixes the precision, rounding mode and exponent range. Each `*_ctx` operation returns the result and the flags it raised. Combine the flags as you go:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:91
msgid "`inexact` tells you that $100/3 \\cdot 3$ was not computed exactly; it is not an error, so `has_error()` stays false. `has_error()` reports invalid operations, division by zero, impossible divisions and invalid contexts. Check the individual flags (`overflow`, `underflow`, `inexact`) when your application cares about them."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:97
msgid "Round money with `quantize`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:99
msgid "`quantize` gives a value the exponent of a template value. Choose the rounding mode in the context; commercial rounding is `HalfUp`, which the shared `RoundingMode` enum does not have, so pass `decimal_rounding`:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:121
msgid "Half-even (\"banker's\") rounding sends the tie `2.345` to the even last digit `4`; half-up sends it away from zero. `quantize` never silently picks another exponent: if the result would need more digits than the precision, you get NaN with `invalid_operation`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:126
msgid "Bound a result from both sides"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:128
msgid "Directed rounding gives guaranteed bounds. Rounding the same quotient toward $-\\infty$ and toward $+\\infty$ brackets the exact value:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:144
msgid "The two results are adjacent decimal32 values, and $1/7$ lies strictly between them."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:147
msgid "Exchange decimal64 bits"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:149
msgid "Interchange formats are what databases, files and other languages exchange. Encode in the encoding your peer expects, and decode with the same one:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:170
msgid "Both encodings keep the exponent, so `19.99` comes back with two decimal places. Bits you did not produce yourself may be non-canonical; keep them in a `DecimalInterchange` and call `canonical()` before comparing bit patterns."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:174
msgid "Call an elementary function"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:176
msgid "Logarithms, exponentials, powers and trigonometric functions are correctly rounded in every rounding mode. They need a context with a bounded exponent range, such as a format preset:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:196
msgid "`try_ln_ctx` returns `Err` only if the result could not be certified within the refinement budget; `ln_ctx` turns that case into NaN with `invalid_operation`. Exact results such as $\\log_{10} 1000 = 3$ come back without `inexact`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:203
msgid "Generic code over the algebra traits"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:205
msgid "`Decimal` implements `Ring` from [luna-generic](https://lunaflow.cn/en/luna-generic/) through its plain operators, so generic code runs on it unchanged:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:225
msgid "The plain operators have no context: `*` is exact, `+` rounds to the larger operand precision (34 digits by default) and returns the shortest cohort member. Code that needs a precision, an exponent range or flags should take a `DecimalContext` and call the `*_ctx` operations."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:230
msgid "The shared contextual traits"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:232
msgid "Code written against [`Luna-Flow/arithmetic`](https://lunaflow.cn/en/arithmetic/) uses `ArithmeticContext` and gets an `ArithmeticOutcome` with diagnostics:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:251
msgid "Errors (`invalid_operation`, `division_by_zero`, …) become `Err`; inexactness and range events become diagnostics."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:254
msgid "Pipelines, GDA status and intervals"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:256
msgid "[`decimal_checked`](decimal_checked.md) wraps a value, its context and its accumulated flags, so a long pipeline does not need explicit `combine` calls."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:259
msgid "[`decimal_gda`](decimal_gda.md) implements the General Decimal Arithmetic model with sticky status and traps. Its values are a separate type; use it when you need `.decTest` behaviour, not IEEE per-operation flags."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:262
msgid "To enclose a decimal value in a binary interval, convert it twice with `to_bin_float(mode=TowardNegative)` and `to_bin_float(mode=TowardPositive)` and build a [`ball_float`](ball_float.md) ball from both bounds."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:266
msgid "A deterministic order for storage"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:268
msgid "`compare_total` orders every representation, including cohorts, signed zeros and NaNs, so it is the right key for sorting stored values or deduplicating bit-exact records:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:284
msgid "**Elementary functions need a bounded context.** `DecimalContext::new()` has the exponent range $\\pm 999\\,999\\,999$, which is outside what the elementary functions accept; they return NaN with `invalid_context`. Use `decimal32()`/`decimal64()`/`decimal128()` or pass `e_min`/`e_max` within $\\pm 999\\,999$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:289
msgid "**Operators are not context operations.** `*` never rounds, so repeated products grow without bound; `/` rounds to the operand precision and can, rarely, be off by one unit in the last place. Use `mul_ctx` and `div_ctx` when the result must be bounded or correctly rounded."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:293
msgid "**`==` is not IEEE equality.** `Eq` and `compare` treat every NaN as equal to every NaN and greater than every number, so sorting works. Use `compare_checked` or `is_nan` when a NaN must be unordered."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:296
msgid "**`has_error()` is narrow.** It ignores `inexact`, `overflow`, `underflow` and `conversion_syntax`. After `from_string_ctx`, check `conversion_syntax` or `is_nan()` to detect bad text."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:299
msgid "**Integer exponents are converted at context precision.** `pown_ctx`, `pow_int_checked` and `pow_nat_checked` turn the integer exponent into a `Decimal` with the context precision, so an exponent with more digits than the precision is rounded before the power is taken. Keep $|n| < 10^{p}$ or call `power_ctx` with an exact `Decimal` exponent."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:304
msgid "**`with_rounding` cannot choose `HalfUp`, `HalfDown` or `ZeroFiveUp`.** Build the context with `DecimalContext::new(decimal_rounding=...)`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:306
msgid "**Binary conversions lose decimal meaning.** `from_double(0.1)` is the exact binary value `0.1000000000000000055511151231257827` (rounded to 34 digits), not `0.1`; parse text instead. `from_bin_float` turns $-0$ into $+0$."
msgstr ""

