#: manual/tutorial/def.md:3
msgid "This tutorial shows how to write code that works with every number type of `floating` at once: you inspect values through the `Floating` trait, test their class with the generic predicates, read IEEE comparison results as `PartialOrder`, and reuse the arithmetic context and error types that `def` re-exports. `def` itself does no arithmetic; the concrete packages ([`bin_float`](bin_float.md), [`decimal`](decimal.md), [`decimal_gda`](decimal_gda.md), [`ball_float`](ball_float.md)) do. The laws behind the trait are in the [design page](../design/def.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/def.md:14
msgid "Add the module and import `def` next to the representation you use:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/def.md:27
msgid "The smallest useful program asks a value what it is:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/def.md:41
msgid "Write one function for every representation"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/def.md:43
msgid "A function bounded by `F : @def.Floating` accepts binary, IEEE decimal, GDA decimal and interval values. Call the trait methods in their qualified form, `@def.Floating::classify(x)`, because MoonBit no longer turns trait methods into dot methods of a type parameter."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/def.md:81
msgid "The interval case shows why the NaN test comes first: the empty interval classifies as `NaN`, and `BallFloat::sign` aborts on it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/def.md:84
msgid "Change precision generically"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/def.md:86
msgid "`with_precision` rounds to the requested number of significant digits of the value's own radix, bits for binary and digits for decimal:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/def.md:104
msgid "Eleven is `1011` in binary; three significant bits round it to `1100`, which `to_string` prints as $3 \\cdot 2^2$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/def.md:107
msgid "Read an IEEE comparison"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/def.md:109
msgid "Comparisons that follow IEEE 754 return a `PartialOrder`, whose fourth value `Unordered` reports a NaN operand:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/def.md:136
msgid "Normalize before comparing representations"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/def.md:138
msgid "Decimal values with the same value can differ in their stored exponent (`1.5` and `1.500` belong to one *cohort*). `normalized` picks the canonical member, so the printed forms agree:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/def.md:158
msgid "Use the re-exported arithmetic types"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/def.md:160
msgid "`@def.ArithmeticContext`, `@def.ArithmeticError`, `@def.RoundingMode` and the other aliases are the types of [Luna-Flow/arithmetic](https://lunaflow.cn/en/arithmetic/). Code that only needs the vocabulary can import `def` instead of `arithmetic`:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/def.md:188
msgid "Implement `Floating` for your own type"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/def.md:190
msgid "The trait is open. A wrapper type can delegate to an existing implementation; it must then keep the laws listed in the design page (for example, `normalized` must not change the value):"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/def.md:238
msgid "`Sign::Zero` does not mean \"the value is zero\". It is also returned for NaN by the scalar types and for any interval that contains zero. Test `@def.is_nan` first, and for intervals use `BallFloat::contains_zero` or the bounds."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/def.md:242
msgid "`@def.is_zero` on a `BallFloat` is true for $[-1, 1]$: the predicate is defined through `sign`, which for an interval means \"contains zero\"."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/def.md:244
msgid "`Sign` cannot tell $-0$ from $+0$. Use the concrete package (`BinFloat::is_negative_zero`, `Decimal::is_signed`) when the sign bit matters."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/def.md:247
msgid "`PartialOrder` is not an ordering you can sort with: `Unordered` breaks trichotomy. Use the total orders of the concrete packages (`BinFloat::total_order`, `Decimal::compare_total`) for sorting."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/def.md:250
msgid "`with_precision` reports no flags and ignores exponent limits. When rounding must be observed, use the `*_ctx` operations of the concrete package."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/def.md:252
msgid "`with_precision(x, 0, mode)` is not an error: the precision is clamped to 1."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/def.md:256
msgid "[`def` design](../design/def.md): the trait laws and the order theory of `PartialOrder`."
msgstr ""

