#: manual/tutorial/frontend/gda_expr.md:1
msgid "gda_expr tutorial"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/gda_expr.md:3
msgid "This tutorial shows how to run General Decimal Arithmetic test rows (`.decTest` files) against `decimal_gda` from MoonBit code: parse a document, execute it, read the summary, and find out why a row failed. It also shows how to select rows and split a run into shards. To run whole corpora from the command line, use the [`gda_expr_cli`](../cli/gda_expr_cli.md) runner instead."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/gda_expr.md:11
#: manual/tutorial/frontend/itl_expr.md:10
#: manual/tutorial/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:11
#: manual/tutorial/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:13
#: manual/tutorial/numeric_expr.md:12
msgid "Add the package to `moon.pkg`:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/gda_expr.md:19
msgid "Parse two rows and execute them:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/gda_expr.md:37
msgid "A row has the form `id operation operand… -> expected condition…`. The directives above the rows (`precision:`, `rounding:`, …) set the context that every following row uses."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/gda_expr.md:43
msgid "Read why a row failed"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/gda_expr.md:45
msgid "A row passes only if both the result and the exact set of conditions (status flags) match. Each `CaseResult` carries a message for a failure:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/gda_expr.md:72
msgid "`bad2` has the right digits but omits `Inexact Rounded`; an extra flag is a failure just like a missing one."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/gda_expr.md:75
msgid "Results are compared with their exponent"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/gda_expr.md:77
msgid "The expected result is compared as a decimal *representation*, not only as a number: `2.0` and `2.00` have the same value but different exponents, and only the one with the right exponent passes. Signed zeros and NaN payloads are compared the same way."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/gda_expr.md:100
msgid "Skipped rows and dispositions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/gda_expr.md:102
msgid "Some rows are not executed. Each result has a `CaseDisposition`: `Executable` rows are run; `Diagnostic` rows (a `#` placeholder operand or result, or a `?` operand) and `Unsupported` rows (an unknown operation, condition or rounding mode) are counted as skipped and never fail the run."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/gda_expr.md:140
msgid "`success()` only says that no executable row failed. A strict runner also requires that nothing was unsupported; the CLI does this with `--strict-supported`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/gda_expr.md:144
msgid "Select rows and split work into shards"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/gda_expr.md:146
msgid "`RunOptions` filters rows by id and selects one shard of the remaining rows. A filter is a comma-separated list of ids or ranges `first..last`, where a range matches ids of the same length that sort between the bounds:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/gda_expr.md:180
msgid "Shard `i` of `n` takes the rows whose position among the filtered rows is congruent to `i` modulo `n`, so the shards are disjoint and together cover every row. `RunSummary::merge` adds the counts back up."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/gda_expr.md:184
msgid "Look at the parsed rows"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/gda_expr.md:186
msgid "`parse_dectest` keeps each row's text and the context in force for it, so you can inspect a corpus without executing it:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/gda_expr.md:212
msgid "**Interchange rows.** In a context whose precision and exponent limits are exactly those of decimal32, decimal64 or decimal128, operands and results written as `#` followed by hexadecimal digits are IEEE 754 interchange encodings, and the result is compared bit for bit. Operands prefixed `32#`, `64#` or `128#` are read in that format."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/gda_expr.md:217
msgid "**Expected `?`.** A result of `?` means \"any value\": only the conditions are compared."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/gda_expr.md:219
msgid "**Your own executor.** Every `GdaCase` exposes `expression()`, a [`numeric_expr`](../numeric_expr.md) tree, so you can evaluate the same rows with your own callbacks, for example against another decimal library."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/gda_expr.md:222
msgid "**Whole corpora.** `just conformance run decimal_gda` downloads the pinned official corpus, builds the native runner and executes all phases; see [verification](../../verification.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/gda_expr.md:228
msgid "**Parse errors reject the whole document.** `parse_dectest` returns all diagnostics and no document if any line is malformed; fix the line or drop it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/gda_expr.md:231
msgid "**The default context is not a decTest default.** Before any directive the context is precision 34, `half_even`, exponents $\\pm 999999999$, `extended: 1`, `clamp: 0`. Real files set their own directives."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/gda_expr.md:234
msgid "**`success()` ignores skipped rows.** Check `unsupported_cases()` (or use the CLI's `--strict-supported`) if unsupported rows must fail the run."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/gda_expr.md:236
msgid "**Doubled quotes.** The tokenizer ends a quoted token at the next matching quote; the `''` escape of the decTest format is read as two tokens."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/gda_expr.md:241
msgid "[gda_expr API](../../api/frontend/gda_expr.md) for every item."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/gda_expr.md:242
msgid "[gda_expr design](../../design/frontend/gda_expr.md) for the exact pass rule and the row-to-operation mapping."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/gda_expr.md:244
msgid "[decimal_gda tutorial](../decimal_gda.md) for the arithmetic being tested."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/itl_expr.md:1
msgid "itl_expr tutorial"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/itl_expr.md:3
msgid "This tutorial shows how to run IEEE 1788 interval test cases written in the ITL format of the ITF1788 suite against `ball_float`. You parse ITL text into cases, execute each case, and summarize the results. The command-line runner for whole files is [`itl_expr_cli`](../cli/itl_expr_cli.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/itl_expr.md:18
msgid "Parse a test case block and run it:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/itl_expr.md:37
msgid "Each statement `operation operand… = expected;` becomes one `ItlCase` with the id `testcase:index`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/itl_expr.md:42
msgid "Inspect parsed cases"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/itl_expr.md:61
msgid "A statement may span several lines; it ends at `;`. Bounds can be decimal or hexadecimal (`0x1.8p1`) text, and the literals `[empty]`, `[entire]` and `[nai]` are understood."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/itl_expr.md:65
msgid "Check decorations"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/itl_expr.md:67
msgid "A decoration suffix (`_com`, `_dac`, `_def`, `_trv`, `_ill`) on the expected value makes the decoration part of the test. Without a suffix only the set is compared. Failure messages print intervals in midpoint-radius form:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/itl_expr.md:90
msgid "Numbers, booleans and overlap states"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/itl_expr.md:92
msgid "Besides interval results, ITL cases can expect a number (`inf`, `sup`, `mid`, `rad`, `wid`, `mag`, `mig`), a boolean (`isEmpty`, `subset`, `isMember`, …) or an overlap state name:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/itl_expr.md:114
msgid "Unsupported and malformed cases"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/itl_expr.md:116
msgid "A case the executor does not implement is `Unsupported`; a case whose operands cannot be read is a `Diagnostic`. Neither counts as a failure, but a diagnostic makes `success()` false:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/itl_expr.md:140
msgid "**Corpus runs.** `just conformance run interval` fetches the pinned ITF1788 corpus, builds the native runner and runs the strict phases listed in `testdata/interval/interpreter_stages.json`; see [verification](../../verification.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/itl_expr.md:144
msgid "**Filtering by operation.** The CLI's `--operation NAME` option keeps only cases of that operation; in code, filter `cases` by `operation()` before executing."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/itl_expr.md:147
msgid "**Precision.** `execute_case(case, precision=p)` parses bounds at `p` bits; the interval operations themselves are rounded to binary64, so keep the default `53` for ITF1788 data."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/itl_expr.md:153
msgid "**Decimal bounds are rounded to nearest.** A bound such as `0.1` is read as the nearest binary64 number, not rounded outward, so `[0.1,0.1]` is the singleton of that binary64 number."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/itl_expr.md:156
msgid "**Signals are not checked.** ITL annotations such as `signal …` after the expected value make the expected value unreadable; such cases become unsupported or diagnostic rather than passing."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/itl_expr.md:159
msgid "**Parse errors reject the input.** `parse_itl` returns only diagnostics if any statement lacks `=` or an operation, or if the text ends inside a statement."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/itl_expr.md:165
msgid "[itl_expr API](../../api/frontend/itl_expr.md) for every item and the list of executed operations."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/itl_expr.md:167
msgid "[itl_expr design](../../design/frontend/itl_expr.md) for the pass rule."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/itl_expr.md:168
msgid "[ball_float tutorial](../ball_float.md) for the interval arithmetic being tested."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:1
msgid "mpfr_expr tutorial"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:3
msgid "This tutorial shows how to check `bin_float` against reference results computed with GNU MPFR. Three line-oriented formats are supported: MPFR's own square-root test data, integer powers, and an elementary-function matrix. You parse a file into a document, execute it, and read the summary. The command-line runner is [`mpfr_expr_cli`](../cli/mpfr_expr_cli.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:19
msgid "Check one square root: $\\sqrt{2^{-1074} \\cdot 2^{52}} = 2^{-511}$ at 53 bits, rounding to nearest:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:36
msgid "Numbers are written as hexadecimal significand and binary exponent: `0x10000000000000p-1074` is $2^{52} \\cdot 2^{-1074}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:41
msgid "Elementary functions with flags"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:43
msgid "An elementary row names the function, the output precision and rounding, the operands, the expected result and three exception flags (inexact, invalid, division by zero). Unused operands are written `-` (second operand) and `0` (integer operand):"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:67
msgid "`ln(0) = -inf` raises division by zero and `sqrt(-1)` is an invalid operation returning NaN; both flags are part of the check."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:72
msgid "A `pow` row splits each number into coefficient, binary exponent and sign:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:87
msgid "The fields are: precision, rounding, input coefficient (hex digits), input exponent, input sign (`1` negative), integer exponent, expected coefficient, expected exponent, expected sign, inexact flag. Here `0x6c0p-9` is $1728/512 = 27/8$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:92
msgid "Read a failure"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:113
msgid "The id is the operation (or `sqrt`/`pow`) and the line number."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:115
msgid "Parse errors"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:117
msgid "Every malformed line is reported with its line number, and no document is returned:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:136
msgid "**Corpora.** `just conformance run binary` executes the pinned MPFR `tests/data/sqrt` file and the committed elementary matrix `testdata/bin_float/mpfr-4.2.2-elementary.txt` together with TestFloat; see [verification](../../verification.md). The generators for the elementary and power data are `tools/generate_mpfr_elementary_oracle.c` and `tools/generate_mpfr_pow_oracle.c`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:142
msgid "**Which parser?** The CLI chooses the elementary format when the file contains `mpfr-elementary-v1`, the power format when it contains `input_coefficient_hex`, and the square-root format otherwise. Put the marker in a comment line."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:149
msgid "**Elementary rows compare numbers, not encodings.** `+0` and `-0` compare equal there, and any NaN matches an expected `nan`. Square-root and power rows compare the whole `BinFloat`, including the sign of zero."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:152
msgid "**Square-root flags are not checked.** `sqrt` data rows compare only the value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:154
msgid "**No exponent range.** Rows are executed in an unbounded context: there is no overflow, underflow or subnormal range, and an elementary or power row that reports overflow or underflow fails."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:157
msgid "**Binary operations need a second operand.** An elementary `pow`, `hypot` or `atan2` row with `-` as second operand is accepted by the parser but aborts when executed."
msgstr ""

