#: manual/tutorial/numeric_expr.md:1
msgid "numeric_expr tutorial"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/numeric_expr.md:3
msgid "This tutorial shows how to describe a computation once as a `numeric_expr` tree and run it against any number type by supplying two callbacks: one that reads a literal and one that executes an operation. By the end you can evaluate expressions over `Int`, over `BinFloat` with IEEE flags, and report failures at their source line. This is the mechanism the conformance frontends (`frontend/gda_expr` and others) use to run test corpora."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/numeric_expr.md:20
msgid "Build `1 + 2` and evaluate it over `Int`:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/numeric_expr.md:41
msgid "`evaluate` decodes both literals, then calls the operation callback with the decoded arguments `[1, 2]`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/numeric_expr.md:46
msgid "Write a small interpreter"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/numeric_expr.md:48
msgid "Most callers match on the operation name and the argument array. Matching the array pattern also checks the arity, which `numeric_expr` itself does not do:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/numeric_expr.md:101
msgid "`sum` shows that an operation may take any number of arguments; the callback decides."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/numeric_expr.md:104
msgid "Evaluate over `BinFloat` and collect IEEE flags"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/numeric_expr.md:106
msgid "The value type `V` can carry more than a number. Here every value is a `BinFloat` together with the IEEE flags raised so far, and each operation merges the flags of its arguments with its own:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/numeric_expr.md:163
msgid "The same tree could be evaluated over `@decimal_gda.Decimal` by swapping the two callbacks; nothing in the expression depends on the number type."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/numeric_expr.md:166
msgid "Report errors at their source"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/numeric_expr.md:168
msgid "Give every node the span of the text it came from. When a callback fails, the `EvalError` returns the failing node, so the span locates the error:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/numeric_expr.md:199
msgid "See the evaluation order"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/numeric_expr.md:201
msgid "The callbacks run in post-order, left to right, and evaluation stops at the first failure. A trace makes this visible:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/numeric_expr.md:227
msgid "The literal `1` and both `add` nodes are never visited, because `bad` fails first."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/numeric_expr.md:232
msgid "**Corpus frontends.** `frontend/gda_expr` lowers each `.decTest` row `id operation operands -> expected conditions` into `Expr::invoke(Operation::new(op), operands.map(Expr::literal))` and evaluates it with callbacks over `@decimal_gda`. Read [gda_expr](frontend/gda_expr.md) for a complete example of the pattern."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/numeric_expr.md:237
msgid "**Richer values.** `V` can be an enum when operations return different kinds of results (numbers, booleans, strings), as `gda_expr` does for `compare`, `class` and `isnan`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/numeric_expr.md:240
msgid "**Typed errors.** `E` can be your own enum instead of `String`; `EvalError[E]` keeps it unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/numeric_expr.md:242
msgid "**Checked pipelines.** If you want domain errors to stay values instead of stopping the evaluation, make `V` a checked type such as `@bin_float_checked.BinFloatResult` and let the callbacks always return `Ok`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/numeric_expr.md:248
msgid "**No arity check.** `Expr::invoke` accepts any number of arguments. Check the arity in `invoke` (an array pattern does it) and return `Err` on a mismatch."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/numeric_expr.md:250
msgid "**Early stop.** After the first failure no further callback runs. Do not rely on side effects of callbacks for nodes to the right of a failing node."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/numeric_expr.md:252
msgid "**Deep trees.** Evaluation is recursive; the stack depth equals the tree height. Very deep left-leaning trees (for example a sum of a million terms built as nested `add`) can exhaust the stack; build a flat `sum` node instead."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/numeric_expr.md:255
msgid "**Default spans.** Without an explicit span a node reports `SourceSpan::new(\"\")`, whose line and column are `0`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/numeric_expr.md:257
msgid "**No literal parsing here.** A `Literal` stores raw text. Rounding, precision and flags of the conversion are decided entirely by your `decode` callback."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/numeric_expr.md:262
msgid "[numeric_expr API](../api/numeric_expr.md) lists every item."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/numeric_expr.md:263
msgid "[numeric_expr design](../design/numeric_expr.md) states the evaluation semantics and its invariants."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/numeric_expr.md:265
msgid "[gda_expr tutorial](frontend/gda_expr.md) runs real decimal test rows through this package."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/semantic.md:1
msgid "`semantic` tutorial"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/semantic.md:3
msgid "This tutorial shows how to compare values across representations without rounding: you project a binary float, an IEEE decimal or an interval onto exact rationals, test equality, order rationals with integer arithmetic, check whether an interval contains a decimal, and turn checked results into a common error vocabulary. The projection is useful in cross-representation tests, diagnostics and protocol boundaries; arithmetic stays in the concrete packages. The mathematics is in the [design page](../design/semantic.md); every item is listed in the [API reference](../api/semantic.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/semantic.md:26
msgid "Three represented as a 32-bit binary float and as a decimal is the same number:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/semantic.md:43
msgid "See the exact value of a float"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/semantic.md:45
msgid "The examples below print a projected scalar with this helper:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/semantic.md:70
msgid "The denominator of the binary value is $2^{55}$; the decimal value is exactly $1/10$. The projections differ, so binary64 `0.1` is not one tenth."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/semantic.md:73
msgid "Ignore cohorts, precision and signed zeros"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/semantic.md:75
msgid "Decimal `1.5`, `1.50` and `1.500` are different representations of one value (a cohort); a binary value carries a precision; zero has two signs. The projection forgets all of this:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/semantic.md:99
msgid "Order two values exactly"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/semantic.md:101
msgid "The package provides equality only. Because a projected rational exposes a reduced numerator and a positive denominator, you can order two of them with the cross-multiplication rule $a/b < c/d \\iff ad < cb$ (valid because $b, d > 0$):"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/semantic.md:124
msgid "Check that an interval encloses a decimal"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/semantic.md:126
msgid "`SemanticInterval::from_ball_float` exposes the exact endpoints of a ball. With the comparison above you can check an enclosure against a decimal reference value without any rounding:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/semantic.md:160
msgid "The empty interval projects to the reversed pair $(+\\infty, -\\infty)$, so the helper rejects every value without a special case."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/semantic.md:165
msgid "Compare checked results across packages"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/semantic.md:167
msgid "`semantic_scalar_result` maps a `Result[T, ArithmeticError]` to a `SemanticResult`, so an error from one package and the same error from another compare equal although their messages differ:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/semantic.md:190
msgid "Cross-check two implementations"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/semantic.md:192
msgid "A typical consistency test computes one result in two representations, rounds both to values that are exactly representable in each, and compares the projections. Square roots of perfect squares are exact in both radices:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/semantic.md:215
msgid "`NaN == NaN` is `true` for `SemanticScalar`. The projection is a value model, not IEEE comparison; use the concrete packages for IEEE predicates."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/semantic.md:217
msgid "The projection drops the sign of zero, NaN payloads and signalling state, decimal cohorts, precision, interval decorations and flags. Do not use it to test those."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/semantic.md:220
msgid "There is no ordering in the package. Write the cross-multiplication yourself as above; never convert to `Double` to compare."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/semantic.md:222
msgid "Projecting a decimal with a huge exponent (for example `1E+999999`) builds a `BigInt` with as many digits as the exponent. Keep projections to values of moderate exponent."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/semantic.md:225
msgid "Only `@decimal.Decimal` has a projection; convert a `@decimal_gda.Decimal` through its string form if you need one."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/semantic.md:227
msgid "`ExactRational::new` aborts on a zero denominator."
msgstr ""

